window.BANK = [{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A bakery buys an industrial oven for $9,000. The seller also installs it for $400. A dispute breaks out, and the court uses the predominant purpose test. Which law governs?","o":["The common law, because installation is a service.","The UCC, because the deal is mostly about buying the oven.","Whichever law the bakery prefers, because it is the buyer.","Neither, because mixed deals are unenforceable."],"a":1,"w":"The predominant purpose test asks what the deal is mainly for. A $9,000 oven with $400 of installation is mainly a sale of goods, so Article 2 of the UCC applies to the whole deal.","id":"d1-1"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A dentist sells a patient a $60 whitening kit and performs a $900 whitening procedure. The procedure injures the patient, who sues for breach. Under the gravamen of the complaint test, which law applies?","o":["The UCC, because a kit changed hands.","The UCC, because dentists are merchants.","The common law, because the complaint is about the service.","The common law, because the kit cost less than $500."],"a":2,"w":"The gravamen test looks at the part of the deal that caused the problem. The complaint is about the procedure, which is a service, so the common law applies.","id":"d1-2"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"cl","q":"Which statement about the Restatement (Second) of Contracts is most accurate?","o":["It is a federal statute that binds every court.","It applies only to sales of goods between merchants.","It was enacted by every state legislature except one.","It is not binding law, but courts treat it as persuasive and often follow it."],"a":3,"w":"The Restatement is a summary of the common law written by the American Law Institute. It is not a statute and binds no one, but judges find it persuasive. The UCC is the one that legislatures enacted.","id":"d1-3"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"At dinner, Sam writes on a napkin, \"I sell my boat to Lee for $8,000,\" signs it, and hands it over. Sam was privately joking. Lee reasonably believed Sam was serious and accepted. What is the result?","o":["A contract, because what counts is what Sam's words and conduct reasonably showed.","No contract, because Sam did not truly intend to sell.","No contract, because napkins are not valid writings.","A contract only if Sam later admits he was serious."],"a":0,"w":"Contract law uses the objective theory of assent. A secret intention does not matter. If a reasonable person would take the words and conduct as a serious offer, it is one.","id":"d1-4"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Which promise is supported by consideration?","o":["A grandmother promises her grandson $1,000 for his birthday.","A neighbour promises $200 to thank someone who rescued her dog last week.","An aunt promises her nephew $5,000 if he gives up vaping until he turns 25, and he does.","A father promises to pay a stranger who has already cared for his adult son."],"a":2,"w":"Consideration is a bargained-for exchange. The nephew gave up something he had a legal right to do, and the aunt asked for exactly that in return for her promise. The others are a gift, past consideration and moral obligation.","id":"d1-5"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Ravi sells a guitar worth $3,000 to a friend for $300. There is no fraud, duress or other defence. Ravi later says the price was too low to count as consideration. What will a court most likely say?","o":["The contract fails because the exchange was unequal.","The contract stands, because courts do not weigh how much consideration is enough.","The contract stands only if the friend pays the difference.","The contract fails because consideration must be fair market value."],"a":1,"w":"Courts generally do not inquire into the adequacy of consideration. People value things differently, and the parties set their own price. A lopsided deal matters only as evidence of a defence such as fraud or duress.","id":"d1-6"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"A wealthy alum phones a museum and promises a large donation because she loved visiting as a child. The museum pencils an \"expected gift\" into next year's budget and does nothing else. She dies before paying. What happens to the museum's claim against her estate?","o":["It succeeds, because charitable pledges are always binding.","It succeeds, because the museum relied by budgeting for the gift.","It fails only because the pledge was oral.","It most likely fails, because there was neither consideration nor real reliance."],"a":3,"w":"A bare promise to make a gift is not enforceable. The museum promised nothing in return, and writing a line in a budget is not the kind of costly reliance that makes a promise binding.","id":"d1-7"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"An offer states: \"To accept, deliver a signed letter to my office by Friday.\" On Thursday the offeree texts, \"I accept.\" Is there a contract?","o":["Yes, because any reasonable method of acceptance works.","Yes, because a text arrives faster than a letter.","No, because the offeror is master of the offer and set the only way to accept.","No, because offers cannot be accepted before the deadline."],"a":2,"w":"The offeror controls the offer. If the offer says acceptance can only be made in one way, following those instructions is the only way to form the contract.","id":"d1-8"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A shop's ad reads: \"Saturday, 9 a.m.: three brand-new espresso machines, $10 each, first come, first served.\" Dana is first in line with $10. The shop refuses to sell. Was the ad an offer?","o":["Yes, because it was clear, definite and left nothing open to negotiate.","No, because advertisements can never be offers.","No, because Dana did not sign anything.","Yes, but only if Dana had phoned ahead."],"a":0,"w":"Ads are usually just invitations to deal. The exception is an ad that is clear, definite and explicit and leaves nothing to negotiate. This one names the item, the price, the quantity and who can accept.","id":"d1-9"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A five-year lease gives the tenant a right to renew \"at a rent to be agreed upon.\" The parties cannot agree, and the tenant asks a court to set a reasonable rent. Under the traditional common-law approach, what is the result?","o":["The court sets the rent at market value.","The renewal clause is an unenforceable agreement to agree.","The old rent continues automatically.","The landlord must accept whatever the tenant offers."],"a":1,"w":"An offer must be certain enough that a court can tell what was promised. Leaving a material term like price for future negotiation makes the clause an agreement to agree, which the common law will not enforce.","id":"d1-10"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A seller offers her car for $5,000. The buyer replies, \"I'll give you $4,500.\" The seller says no. The buyer then says, \"Fine, I accept at $5,000.\" Is there a contract?","o":["Yes, because the buyer accepted the original price.","Yes, because the seller never formally revoked.","No, because offers expire after one reply.","No, because the counteroffer rejected the original offer and ended the power to accept it."],"a":3,"w":"Under the mirror image rule, a counteroffer is a rejection. It ends the offeree's power to accept. The buyer's last statement is a new offer that the seller is free to accept or ignore.","id":"d1-11"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner tells a neighbour, \"I'll sell you my lot for $40,000. The offer is open until Friday.\" The neighbour pays nothing for that promise. On Wednesday the neighbour learns from the homeowner's agent that the lot has been sold to someone else. On Thursday the neighbour tries to accept. What is the result?","o":["A contract, because the offer was promised open until Friday.","A contract, because the homeowner never told the neighbour directly.","No contract, because the offer was revoked when the neighbour learned of the sale from a reliable source.","No contract, because offers to sell land cannot be accepted orally."],"a":2,"w":"A promise to keep an offer open is not binding without consideration, so the offeror can revoke any time before acceptance. Revocation can be indirect: the offeror acts inconsistently with the offer, and the offeree hears of it from a reliable source.","id":"d1-12"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"On Monday an offeree posts a properly addressed letter accepting an offer. On Tuesday the offeror's letter revoking the offer reaches the offeree. On Wednesday the acceptance reaches the offeror. Was a contract formed?","o":["Yes, on Monday, because acceptance takes effect when sent.","No, because the revocation arrived before the acceptance did.","Yes, but only on Wednesday when the acceptance arrived.","No, because posted acceptances are never effective."],"a":0,"w":"Under the mailbox rule, an acceptance is effective on dispatch. A revocation is effective only when received. The contract formed on Monday, so Tuesday's revocation came too late.","id":"d1-13"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A widow writes to her daughter: \"If you move here and care for me for the rest of my life, the house is yours when I die.\" The daughter moves and begins caring for her. Weeks later they quarrel and the widow tries to withdraw. What best describes the situation?","o":["No contract, because the daughter never promised anything.","A unilateral offer that the daughter accepted by performing, so the widow cannot simply withdraw.","A gift promise that can be revoked at any time.","A bilateral contract that failed for lack of a signature."],"a":1,"w":"The offer asked for performance, not a return promise. Once the offeree has begun the requested performance, the modern rule protects her reliance and the offer can no longer be freely revoked.","id":"d1-14"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT an element of promissory estoppel under the Restatement?","o":["A promise.","Reliance that the promisor should reasonably have expected.","Injustice that can be avoided only by enforcing the promise.","A signed writing recording the promise."],"a":3,"w":"Promissory estoppel needs a promise, reliance the promisor should reasonably expect, actual reliance to the promisee's detriment, and injustice without enforcement. No writing is required. That is the point: it rescues promises that fail as formal contracts.","id":"d1-15"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"A ferry ticket's printed terms require every lawsuit to be filed in the ferry company's home state. A passenger who had notice of the terms is injured and sues at home instead. Under the leading approach to such clauses, what is the result?","o":["The clause is void because the passenger could not negotiate it.","The clause is void because consumers never have to read tickets.","The clause is generally enforceable if it is reasonable and the passenger had notice.","The clause is enforceable only between two businesses."],"a":2,"w":"Forum selection clauses in form contracts are generally enforced when they are reasonable and the customer had notice, even without bargaining. Courts point to the savings from having disputes heard in one place.","id":"d2-1"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"What is a contract of adhesion?","o":["A standard form offered on a take-it-or-leave-it basis.","A contract that both parties negotiated clause by clause.","A contract that is automatically void.","A contract that must be notarised."],"a":0,"w":"An adhesion contract is a standard form drafted by one side and offered with no room to negotiate. It is not automatically unenforceable, but its hidden or one-sided terms get closer scrutiny.","id":"d2-2"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"A website's terms are available only through a small grey link at the very bottom of the page. A visitor downloads free software by clicking \"Download\" and is never asked to agree to anything. Is the visitor bound by an arbitration clause in those terms?","o":["Yes, because everyone has a duty to read.","Likely not, because a reasonable user would not have known the terms existed.","Yes, because downloading is always acceptance.","Likely not, because arbitration clauses are illegal online."],"a":1,"w":"This is browsewrap. Clicking a button is not assent to terms the user had no reasonable notice of. The duty to read does not reach terms that a reasonably prudent user would never have seen.","id":"d2-3"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"To create an account, a user must tick a box next to \"I agree to the Terms of Service,\" where the words \"Terms of Service\" are a clear blue link. The user ticks the box without reading. Is the user bound by the terms?","o":["No, because the user never read them.","No, because online terms need a handwritten signature.","Only if the user later uses the service for a year.","Yes, because the user had notice and took a clear step showing agreement."],"a":3,"w":"This is clickwrap. A person who has notice of the terms and takes a clear affirmative step to agree is bound, whether or not they actually read them. That is the duty to read.","id":"d2-4"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"For passive online assent, which statement about notice is most accurate?","o":["Only actual knowledge of each term will do.","Notice is irrelevant if the service is free.","Inquiry notice can be enough: the user need only be put on notice that terms exist.","Notice matters only for business users."],"a":2,"w":"Actual notice works, but so does inquiry notice. If the design would alert a reasonably prudent user that terms apply, the user is expected to look them up.","id":"d2-5"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"cl","q":"Under the common law, a buyer sends a purchase order and the seller replies with an acknowledgment containing different terms. The seller ships and the buyer accepts the goods. Whose terms control?","o":["The seller's, because the last form sent before performance wins.","The buyer's, because the first form sent always wins.","Neither, because the forms did not match.","Whichever form is longer."],"a":0,"w":"At common law the mismatched reply is a counteroffer, and performing accepts it. The terms of the last form sent before performance control. This is the last shot rule, which is what the UCC set out to replace.","id":"d2-6"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A merchant buyer orders 500 chairs on its purchase order. The merchant seller's acknowledgment agrees to the order but adds, \"1% monthly interest on late payments.\" It does not make acceptance conditional on that term. Under the UCC, is there a contract?","o":["No, because the acknowledgment was not a mirror image.","Yes, because a definite expression of acceptance works even with an additional term.","No, because interest terms are always material.","Yes, but only once the chairs are delivered."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-207(1) drops the mirror image rule for goods. A definite and timely acceptance forms a contract even if it adds or changes terms, unless it is expressly made conditional on assent to those terms.","id":"d2-7"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"Between merchants, an additional term in an acceptance becomes part of the contract unless an exception applies. Which of the following is NOT one of the exceptions?","o":["The offer expressly limited acceptance to its own terms.","The additional term would materially alter the contract.","The offeror objects to the term within a reasonable time.","The additional term was printed in a smaller font than the rest."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 2-207(2), additional terms between merchants come in unless the offer limited acceptance to its terms, the term materially alters the deal, or the offeror objects. Font size is not on the list.","id":"d2-8"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer's form says the seller gives full implied warranties. The seller's form says all implied warranties are disclaimed. The forms otherwise make a contract. Under the knockout rule most courts apply, what is the result?","o":["The seller's disclaimer controls, because it came last.","The buyer's term controls, because it came first.","Both terms drop out, and the UCC's default rules fill the gap.","The whole contract fails."],"a":2,"w":"Under the knockout rule, conflicting terms cancel each other and the UCC's gap fillers take over. The gap filler here is that implied warranties exist, so the buyer ends up protected.","id":"d2-9"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A gym's form contract says a \"fee may apply upon early termination.\" The clause can reasonably be read two ways. Why would a court read it against the gym?","o":["Because the party who drafted the words should not benefit from their vagueness.","Because consumers always win contract disputes.","Because ambiguous contracts are void.","Because gyms are held to a fiduciary standard."],"a":0,"w":"This is contra proferentem: ambiguity is resolved against the drafter. The rule pushes the party who controls the wording to write clearly and not to hide behind obscure language.","id":"d2-10"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A contract calls for delivery of \"salmon.\" The buyer says the word meant wild-caught only. The seller says it covers farmed salmon too. Both readings are plausible. Who has to prove what?","o":["The seller must prove the broader meaning beyond doubt.","The buyer, who argues for the narrower meaning, must show the contract used the word that way.","Neither; the court splits the difference.","The contract is void for vagueness."],"a":1,"w":"The party claiming a word was used in a narrower sense carries the burden of showing it, through the contract's language, negotiations, trade usage and the like. If the evidence is balanced, that party loses.","id":"d2-11"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A written contract for the sale of a house says it is the parties' complete and final agreement. The buyer wants to testify that, before signing, the seller orally promised to leave the grand piano. What is the likely result?","o":["The testimony comes in, because oral promises are always enforceable.","The testimony comes in, because pianos are goods.","The testimony is barred only if the seller objects in writing.","The testimony is barred, because a completely integrated writing displaces earlier agreements within its scope."],"a":3,"w":"When a writing is a complete integration, earlier oral agreements on the same subject cannot be used to add to or contradict it. A promise about what stays in the house is something you would expect to find in the written deal.","id":"d2-12"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Which kind of evidence does the parol evidence rule NOT keep out?","o":["Evidence of an earlier oral term that contradicts the writing.","Evidence of an earlier draft with a different price.","Evidence that the contract was induced by fraud.","Evidence of an earlier side promise within the writing's scope."],"a":2,"w":"The rule guards the terms of a final writing. It does not stop a party from showing the agreement is invalid or voidable, for example because of fraud, duress or mistake. Evidence offered to explain an ambiguous term also comes in.","id":"d2-13"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"both","q":"Which of these agreements falls within the statute of frauds and therefore needs a signed writing?","o":["An oral agreement to sell a parcel of land for $2,000.","An oral agreement to paint a house next week for $5,000.","An oral sale of a used bicycle for $300.","An oral agreement to work as a consultant for six months."],"a":0,"w":"Sales of land are within the statute whatever the price. The painting and consulting deals can be performed within a year, and the bicycle is under the $500 line for goods.","id":"d2-14"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"An employer orally hires a manager for two years. There is no single signed contract, but there is an unsigned memo of the terms and two signed payroll forms that refer to the same hiring. Can the statute of frauds be satisfied?","o":["No, because the statute requires one signed document.","Yes, because several writings can be read together when they clearly relate to the same deal.","No, because payroll forms are never contracts.","Yes, because employment contracts are exempt."],"a":1,"w":"The required memorandum can be pieced together from several writings, signed and unsigned, as long as they clearly refer to the same transaction and at least one is signed by the party being charged.","id":"d2-15"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"A harsh clause is buried in tiny print, in a language the buyer cannot read, and the salesperson rushes the signing. Which kind of unconscionability do these facts mainly show?","o":["Substantive, because the clause is harsh.","Neither, because the buyer signed.","Procedural, because the bargaining process was unfair.","Statutory, because the print was small."],"a":2,"w":"Procedural unconscionability is about how the deal was made: hidden terms, pressure, no real choice. Substantive unconscionability is about the terms themselves being grossly one-sided. Most courts want some of each.","id":"d3-1"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"A store sells a freezer worth $300 for $1,300 on credit. The sale is negotiated in the buyer's language, but the contract is in one he cannot read, and he was told the freezer would cost him almost nothing. The store sues for the full price. What can a court do?","o":["Refuse to enforce the unconscionable price and limit the store to a fair amount.","Nothing, because courts never look at price.","Order the buyer to pay double as a penalty.","Enforce the price but cancel the delivery."],"a":0,"w":"Under UCC section 2-302, a court may refuse to enforce an unconscionable term or limit its effect. A gross price markup combined with unfair surprise fits. The seller can still recover the reasonable value of what it delivered.","id":"d3-2"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A seller and a buyer both believe a violin is an ordinary copy and agree on $500. It turns out to be a rare original worth $500,000. The contract did not assign the risk to either side. Can the seller get out?","o":["No, because a deal is a deal.","Yes, because both were mistaken about a basic assumption that materially changed the exchange.","No, because only buyers can claim mistake.","Yes, but only if the buyer agrees."],"a":1,"w":"Mutual mistake makes a contract voidable when both parties were wrong about a basic assumption, the mistake materially affects the exchange, and the party seeking relief did not bear the risk.","id":"d3-3"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"At a flea market a buyer says, \"I can't tell if this ring is gold or brass, but I'll take my chances for $50.\" It is brass. Can the buyer undo the sale for mistake?","o":["Yes, because the ring was worth less than the price.","Yes, because the seller should have tested it.","No, because mistake never applies to jewellery.","No, because the buyer knew his knowledge was limited and went ahead anyway."],"a":3,"w":"A party bears the risk of a mistake when he knows he has only limited knowledge and treats it as good enough. That is conscious ignorance, and it blocks the defence.","id":"d3-4"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A divorcing couple split their assets. One spouse keeps an investment account both believe is worth $5 million. Two years later the fund manager is exposed as a fraudster and the account is worthless. The spouse seeks to redo the settlement for mutual mistake. What is the likely result?","o":["Relief granted, because the spouse lost money.","Relief granted, because settlements must always be equal.","Relief denied, because the account existed and the loss was a later change in the value of a risky asset.","Relief denied, because divorces are not contracts."],"a":2,"w":"Mutual mistake is about facts as they existed when the contract was made, not about how an asset later performs. The spouse who chose the account took the risk of its value going down, just as he would have kept any gain.","id":"d3-5"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A contractor bids $20,000 on a job that obviously costs about $200,000, because of an arithmetic slip. The owner spots the error and quickly accepts. Can the contractor avoid the contract?","o":["Yes, because the owner knew or should have known of the mistake.","No, because one-sided mistakes never count.","No, because bids are irrevocable.","Yes, but only if the contractor pays a penalty."],"a":0,"w":"A unilateral mistake about a basic assumption with a material effect allows avoidance when the other party knew or had reason to know of it, or when enforcement would be unconscionable.","id":"d3-6"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A person is told she is signing up for a free \"personality survey.\" The form is actually a contract with an arbitration clause, and nothing would alert a reasonable person to that. What is the status of the agreement?","o":["Valid, because she signed.","Void, because she did not know she was entering a contract at all.","Voidable, but only by the company.","Valid, because arbitration clauses are favoured."],"a":1,"w":"This is fraud in the inception. The signer does not even know the document is a contract, so there is no assent and the agreement is void. Fraud in the inducement, where she knows it is a contract but is lied to about the deal, makes it voidable.","id":"d3-7"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A seller tells a buyer, \"This roof has never leaked,\" knowing it leaked twice last winter. The buyer relies on the statement and buys the house. What can the buyer do?","o":["Nothing, because of caveat emptor.","Nothing, because the statement was only an opinion.","Avoid the contract only if the roof leaks again.","Avoid the contract, because it was induced by a fraudulent misrepresentation the buyer justifiably relied on."],"a":3,"w":"A knowingly false statement of fact, made to induce the deal and justifiably relied on, makes the contract voidable by the deceived party. This was a statement of fact, not opinion or sales talk.","id":"d3-8"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Which statement is most likely to support a misrepresentation claim?","o":["A pizzeria advertises \"the best slice in the galaxy.\"","A car dealer says, \"You'll love how this one drives.\"","A dance instructor with expert knowledge tells a hopeless student she has professional talent, to sell her 2,000 hours of lessons.","A realtor says a neighbourhood \"has great energy.\""],"a":2,"w":"Opinions and puffery are usually not actionable. The exception is an opinion from someone with superior knowledge who knows it is false and uses it to induce the deal. The other three are ordinary sales talk.","id":"d3-9"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A band rents a concert hall for a Saturday show. On Thursday the hall burns down through no one's fault. The band sues the owner for breach. What is the result?","o":["Both sides are excused, because the hall's continued existence was a basic assumption of the contract.","The owner is liable, because promises must be kept.","The owner must build a new hall.","The band must still pay the rental fee."],"a":0,"w":"When something essential to performance is destroyed without either party's fault, performance is impossible and both are excused. The contract assumed the hall would still exist.","id":"d3-10"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A fan rents a balcony for one afternoon solely to watch a parade, as the owner knows. The parade is cancelled. The balcony is still available. Which doctrine may excuse the fan from paying?","o":["Impossibility, because the balcony cannot be used.","Frustration of purpose, because performance is possible but its whole point is gone.","Duress, because the fan feels pressured.","None, because the balcony still exists."],"a":1,"w":"Frustration of purpose applies when an unforeseen event destroys the principal purpose of the contract, which both parties understood, even though performance remains possible.","id":"d3-11"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A supplier's material costs rise 15% because of ordinary market movement. The supplier wants to be excused for impracticability. What is the likely result?","o":["Excused, because the contract is now less profitable.","Excused, because price changes are always unforeseeable.","Excused, but only for half the contract.","Not excused, because a normal increase in cost is a risk the supplier took on."],"a":3,"w":"Impracticability needs an unexpected event whose non-occurrence was a basic assumption of the deal. Ordinary swings in price or cost are the kind of risk a fixed-price contract allocates.","id":"d3-12"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"A 16-year-old buys a used car, drives it for a month and wrecks it. She wants to return what is left of it and get her money back. What is the general rule?","o":["She is bound, because she used the car.","She is bound, because the seller acted fairly.","She may disaffirm, because a minor's contract is voidable at the minor's option.","The contract was void from the start, so neither side has any rights."],"a":2,"w":"A minor's contract is voidable, not void: the minor may choose to disaffirm or to go through with it. The main exceptions are necessaries and specific statutes. A car is generally not a necessary.","id":"d3-13"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A parts supplier under an existing contract threatens to stop deliveries unless the buyer agrees to pay more. The buyer cannot get the parts anywhere else in time to meet its own deadlines and gives in. What is the status of the price increase?","o":["Voidable for economic duress: a wrongful threat left the buyer with no reasonable alternative.","Binding, because the buyer agreed.","Binding, because businesses cannot claim duress.","Void, because all modifications are illegal."],"a":0,"w":"Economic duress needs an improper threat and no reasonable alternative. Threatening to breach an existing contract to extract more money, when the victim cannot get the goods elsewhere in time, qualifies.","id":"d3-14"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Which fact is NOT one of the usual warning signs of undue influence?","o":["The deal was pushed at an unusual time and place.","The agreed price was higher than the market price.","Several people worked together to persuade one person.","The person was told there was no time to consult a lawyer."],"a":1,"w":"The warning signs are about over-persuasion of a vulnerable person: odd time or place, insistence on finishing at once, emphasis on the cost of delay, multiple persuaders, and no independent advice. A high price alone is not one of them.","id":"d3-15"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner hires a painter for $5,000. The job would cost the painter $3,000 in labour and paint. The homeowner cancels before the painter spends anything. What are the painter's expectation damages?","o":["$5,000","$3,000","$2,000","$0"],"a":2,"w":"Expectation damages put the injured party where performance would have. The painter would have received $5,000 and spent $3,000, for a $2,000 profit. She saved the $3,000 by not doing the job.","id":"d4-1"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"An employee has a one-year contract at $120,000, terminable only for good cause. After nine months and $90,000 in pay, she is fired without cause. Despite reasonable efforts she cannot find other work. What are her expectation damages?","o":["$30,000","$120,000","$90,000","$10,000"],"a":0,"w":"She was promised $120,000 and received $90,000. The benefit of her bargain is the remaining $30,000. Because she made reasonable efforts to find other work, nothing is deducted.","id":"d4-2"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A promoter cannot prove with any certainty what profit a cancelled event would have made. After signing, though, the promoter spent $40,000 preparing for it. Which measure of damages is the promoter most likely to recover?","o":["Expectation damages for the lost profit.","Reliance damages of $40,000.","Punitive damages.","Nothing at all."],"a":1,"w":"When lost profits are too speculative, the fallback is reliance: the out-of-pocket costs incurred because of the promise. They must still be proved, and they must have been spent after the contract was made.","id":"d4-3"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"Which remedy focuses on making the defendant give back a benefit it unjustly received, and not on the plaintiff's lost profit or expenses?","o":["Expectation damages.","Reliance damages.","Liquidated damages.","Restitution."],"a":3,"w":"Restitution measures the defendant's gain. It prevents unjust enrichment, and it can be available even when there is no enforceable contract.","id":"d4-4"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A brand-new company with no sales history claims $10 million in lost profits from a licensing breach. Its only support is its own optimistic projection. Which limit on damages is the biggest obstacle?","o":["Foreseeability.","Mitigation.","Certainty.","Privity."],"a":2,"w":"Damages must be proved with reasonable certainty. A new business is not automatically barred, but it needs a real basis for its numbers, such as a track record or comparable data. A hopeful projection is speculation.","id":"d4-5"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A courier delivers a factory's replacement part two days late. The factory was shut the whole time and lost profits. The courier was never told the factory could not run without the part. Can the factory recover the lost profits?","o":["No, because those losses were not foreseeable to the courier when the contract was made.","Yes, because the courier breached.","Yes, because all losses from a breach are recoverable.","No, because couriers are never liable for damages."],"a":0,"w":"Consequential damages are recoverable only if they were foreseeable at the time of contracting, either because they arise naturally or because the breaching party knew of the special circumstances.","id":"d4-6"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A county cancels a contract for a bridge before much work is done. The builder ignores the cancellation, finishes the bridge and sues for the full price. What is the result?","o":["The builder recovers the full price.","The builder cannot recover the costs it ran up after the cancellation.","The builder recovers nothing at all.","The county must use the bridge."],"a":1,"w":"This is mitigation, the doctrine of avoidable consequences. Once the other side repudiates, you cannot keep working and pile up damages. The builder gets its costs up to the repudiation plus the profit it would have made.","id":"d4-7"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A contract for a $20,000 job says the contractor will pay $50,000 for every day of delay. What will a court most likely do with that clause?","o":["Enforce it, because the parties agreed to it.","Enforce it, but only for the first day.","Double it if the delay was deliberate.","Refuse to enforce it as a penalty."],"a":3,"w":"A liquidated damages clause is enforceable only if the amount is a reasonable estimate of the likely loss. A sum wildly out of proportion to the contract is a penalty, and contract law does not punish.","id":"d4-8"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"In which case is a court most likely to order specific performance?","o":["A contract to buy 100 standard office chairs.","A contract for a singer to perform at a wedding.","A contract to sell a particular parcel of land.","A contract to deliver ordinary gravel."],"a":2,"w":"Specific performance is for cases where money is inadequate. Land is treated as unique. Ordinary goods can be replaced on the market, and courts will not force a person to perform personal services.","id":"d4-9"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A builder innocently installs a different brand of wiring that is identical in quality to the brand the contract named. Replacing it would mean tearing out finished walls, at a cost far above any difference in the house's value. What can the owner recover?","o":["The difference in value, which is close to nothing.","The full cost of tearing out and replacing the wiring.","A refund of the whole contract price.","Nothing, because there was no breach at all."],"a":0,"w":"The builder substantially performed. When the breach is innocent and trivial and the cost of fixing it is grossly out of proportion to the benefit, damages are measured by the difference in value, not the cost of completion.","id":"d4-10"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A café orders 200 blue mugs. The seller delivers 190 blue mugs and 10 green ones. Under the UCC's perfect tender rule, what may the café do?","o":["It must accept everything, because the seller substantially performed.","It may reject all, accept all, or accept some commercial units and reject the rest.","It may only reject the green mugs.","It must keep the mugs and sue later."],"a":1,"w":"For a single delivery of goods, the buyer may reject if the goods fail to conform in any respect. Section 2-601 gives three choices: reject the whole, accept the whole, or accept any commercial units and reject the rest.","id":"d4-11"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A seller delivers nonconforming goods a week before the contract's delivery deadline. The buyer rejects them. The seller promptly says it will deliver conforming goods before the deadline. What are the seller's rights?","o":["None, because rejection ends the contract.","None, because a seller gets only one chance.","The seller may raise the price for the second delivery.","The seller has a right to cure within the contract time."],"a":3,"w":"The perfect tender rule is softened by the seller's right to cure. If the time for performance has not run out, a seller who gives notice may make a conforming delivery within the contract period.","id":"d4-12"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer contracts for 100 units at $10 each. The seller fails to deliver. The buyer promptly and reasonably buys substitutes at $14 each. Ignoring incidental costs, what can the buyer recover?","o":["$1,000","$1,400","$400","$0"],"a":2,"w":"A buyer who covers recovers the cover price minus the contract price. That is $1,400 minus $1,000, or $400. A buyer who does not cover can use the market price in place of the cover price.","id":"d4-13"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"Ana owes Cal $300. Ana lends Ben $300 on Ben's promise to repay it directly to Cal. Ben does not pay. Can Cal sue Ben?","o":["Yes, because Ben's promise was made for Cal's benefit.","No, because Cal was not a party to the contract.","No, because Cal gave no consideration to Ben.","Yes, but only if Ana joins the lawsuit."],"a":0,"w":"A third party may enforce a promise that the contracting parties intended to benefit her. A promise to pay the promisee's creditor is the classic example, even though the creditor is not in privity and gave no consideration.","id":"d4-14"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"both","q":"A famous portrait painter is commissioned to paint a family portrait. Without asking, she hands the job to her student. Can the family refuse the student's work?","o":["No, because duties can always be delegated.","Yes, because the job depended on her personal skill and cannot be delegated without consent.","No, because the student is cheaper.","Yes, but only if the contract was in writing."],"a":1,"w":"Most duties can be delegated, but not those that depend on the particular person's skill, judgment or reputation. The family bargained for this painter, so they need not accept a substitute.","id":"d4-15"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"Article 2 of the UCC applies to transactions in goods. Which of these deals is a sale of goods?","o":["Mo sells his condominium to a co-worker.","Mo pays a barber $40 for a haircut.","Mo hires a tutor for ten algebra lessons.","Mo sells his used taco truck to a neighbour."],"a":3,"w":"Under UCC section 2-105, goods are things that are movable at the time they are identified to the contract. A truck is movable, so its sale is covered by Article 2. Land and services are left to the common law.","id":"b1"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"ucc","q":"Several UCC rules apply only to a \"merchant.\" Who is most clearly a merchant in the sale described?","o":["A bicycle shop owner selling a bicycle from her shop floor.","A dentist selling his old sofa through an online listing.","A retiree selling her late husband's fishing rod at a yard sale.","A student selling last year's laptop to a roommate."],"a":0,"w":"Under UCC section 2-104, a merchant is someone who deals in goods of the kind or who holds themselves out as having special knowledge or skill about them. The bicycle shop owner sells bicycles for a living. The others are casual, one-time sellers.","id":"b2"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"A moving crew agrees to move a family's furniture for $2,000. Halfway through, with nothing unexpected having happened, the crew says it will not finish unless the family promises an extra $500 for the very same job. The family says, \"Fine.\" The move is completed. Under the traditional pre-existing duty rule, can the crew collect the extra $500?","o":["Yes, because the family agreed out loud.","No, because the crew gave nothing new in exchange for the promise of more money.","Yes, because finishing the move was a benefit to the family.","No, because contracts can never be modified once work begins."],"a":1,"w":"Under the pre-existing duty rule in Restatement section 73, doing what you are already legally bound to do is not consideration. The crew promised nothing new in return for the extra $500, so the family's promise to pay it is not enforceable.","id":"b3"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Which of these is an illusory promise that cannot serve as consideration?","o":["\"I will buy all the flour my bakery requires this year from you.\"","\"I will pay you $50 if it rains on Tuesday.\"","\"I will buy as many of your widgets as I may feel like ordering, if any.\"","\"I will paint your fence next week for $300.\""],"a":2,"w":"A promise is illusory when the person making it keeps complete freedom to do nothing at all. Under Restatement section 77, that is not a real commitment and it is not consideration. A requirements promise and a conditional promise both limit the promisor's freedom, so they count.","id":"b4"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Gus tells his neighbour Pat, \"I'm getting rid of my old guitar. If you stop by my garage on Saturday, it's yours for free.\" Pat walks over on Saturday. Gus has changed his mind. Pat argues that walking over was consideration. What is the most accurate statement?","o":["There is no consideration, because stopping by was only a condition of receiving a gift and not something Gus bargained for.","There is consideration, because Pat suffered the detriment of walking over.","There is consideration, because Gus used the word \"if.\"","There is no consideration, because guitars cannot be given away orally."],"a":0,"w":"Consideration under Restatement section 71 must be bargained for, meaning the promisor is seeking it in exchange for the promise. Gus did not want Pat's walk. It was just the step needed to collect a gift, so this is a conditional gift promise and not a bargain.","id":"b5"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Lena honestly believes a caterer overcharged her and tells the caterer she plans to sue. The caterer promises to pay her $400 if she drops the matter, and she agrees. It later turns out her claim would have lost in court. Is the caterer's promise supported by consideration?","o":["No, because giving up a losing claim is giving up nothing.","No, because settlements must be approved by a judge.","Yes, but only if Lena had already filed the lawsuit.","Yes, because Lena gave up a claim she believed in good faith might be valid."],"a":3,"w":"Under Restatement section 74, giving up a claim that turns out to be invalid is still consideration if the claim was doubtful or the person honestly believed it might be valid. Lena's good faith belief is enough. Otherwise almost no settlement would ever be safe.","id":"b6"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Kim writes to a shop owner, \"Will you sell me your shop for $60,000 cash?\" The owner writes back, \"I could not possibly sell it for less than $80,000.\" Kim replies, \"I accept at $80,000.\" The owner refuses to sell. Is there a contract?","o":["Yes, because Kim agreed to the owner's stated price.","Yes, because the owner's reply was in writing.","No, because the owner's reply was an invitation to negotiate and not an offer.","No, because Kim's first letter was a rejection."],"a":2,"w":"An offer must show a willingness to be bound if the other person simply says yes. Saying you would not sell for less than a certain price only signals where bargaining might start. Under Restatement section 26, that is preliminary negotiation, so Kim's reply was itself the offer.","id":"b7"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Dee posts flyers offering a $500 reward for the return of her lost parrot. Raj has never seen or heard of the flyers. He finds the parrot, reads the address on its leg band, and brings it home to Dee out of kindness. He later learns of the reward and demands it as a matter of contract. What is the result?","o":["Raj wins, because he did exactly what the flyer asked.","Raj loses, because he did not know of the offer when he performed, so he could not have accepted it.","Raj wins, because reward offers are binding on everyone in the world.","Raj loses, because reward offers must be accepted in writing."],"a":1,"w":"An offer can only be accepted by someone who knows about it. Raj returned the parrot without any knowledge of the reward, so his act was not given in exchange for Dee's promise. This follows the rule in Restatement sections 23 and 51 that the offeree must know of the offer.","id":"b8"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following does NOT terminate an offeree's power to accept an ordinary offer?","o":["The offeree rejects the offer.","A reasonable time passes without acceptance.","The offeror dies before the offer is accepted.","The offeree asks whether the offeror might consider a lower price, while keeping the offer under consideration."],"a":3,"w":"Under Restatement section 36, the power of acceptance ends by rejection or counteroffer, lapse of time, revocation, or the death or incapacity of either party. A mere inquiry about other terms is not a rejection or a counteroffer, so the original offer stays open.","id":"b9"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A stranger mails Bo an unrequested set of steak knives with a note: \"If we do not hear from you within ten days, you have agreed to buy these for $90.\" Bo puts the box in a closet, never uses the knives, and says nothing. Is Bo bound to pay?","o":["No, because silence is generally not acceptance and Bo had no duty to speak.","Yes, because the note clearly stated how to reject.","Yes, because Bo kept the box in his home.","No, because offers sent by mail are never valid."],"a":0,"w":"Under Restatement section 69, silence counts as acceptance only in narrow cases, such as when the offeree takes the benefit of services or when prior dealings make it reasonable to expect a reply. An offeror cannot force a stranger into a contract just by announcing that silence means yes.","id":"b10"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"Tia pays a landowner $100 for a written option to buy a field for $50,000, good for 30 days. On day 29 she mails a letter exercising the option. The letter arrives on day 32. The option says nothing about how to accept. Under the Restatement, was the option exercised in time?","o":["Yes, because acceptance is effective when mailed.","Yes, because she paid for the option.","No, because an acceptance under an option contract is effective only when the offeror receives it.","No, because options to buy land cannot be accepted by mail."],"a":2,"w":"The mailbox rule protects an offeree from a revocation while her letter is in the post. An option holder does not need that protection, because the offer cannot be revoked. So under Restatement section 63, an acceptance under an option contract takes effect only on receipt.","id":"b11"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"On Monday, Otto mails Fern an offer to sell his canoe for $700. On Tuesday, Otto dies. On Wednesday, Fern, who has no idea Otto has died, mails a letter of acceptance. Under the traditional Restatement rule, is there a contract with Otto's estate?","o":["Yes, because Fern accepted without knowing of the death.","No, because the death of the offeror ended Fern's power to accept, whether or not she knew of it.","Yes, because the mailbox rule made the acceptance effective on Wednesday.","No, because canoes are not goods."],"a":1,"w":"Under Restatement section 48, an offeree's power of acceptance ends when the offeror dies, even if the offeree has not heard the news. The mailbox rule cannot help, because by Wednesday there was no longer an offer to accept. An option contract would be different.","id":"b12"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A furniture dealer sends a restaurant a signed letter: \"We offer 40 oak tables at $300 each. We promise to hold this offer open for six months.\" The restaurant pays nothing for that promise. In month four, the dealer telephones to withdraw the offer. A week later the restaurant tries to accept. What is the result?","o":["A contract, because the dealer promised six months in a signed writing.","No contract, because a firm offer without consideration is irrevocable for no more than three months, so the revocation was effective.","A contract, because merchants can never revoke offers.","No contract, because firm offers are valid only if the buyer also signs."],"a":1,"w":"Under UCC section 2-205, a merchant's signed written promise to hold an offer open is binding without consideration, but only for the time stated and never longer than three months. After three months the dealer was free to revoke, and it did so before the restaurant accepted.","id":"b13"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A school orders 100 red calculators \"for prompt shipment.\" The seller is out of red, so it ships 100 blue calculators with a note: \"We had no red. We are sending blue as an accommodation in case you can use them.\" The school does not want blue. What is the most accurate statement?","o":["The seller accepted the order and breached by shipping the wrong colour.","The seller accepted the order, and the school must keep the blue calculators.","No contract was possible, because an order can be accepted only by a promise.","The shipment was a counteroffer and not an acceptance, so the school may refuse it and the seller is not in breach."],"a":3,"w":"Under UCC section 2-206, an order for prompt shipment can be accepted by shipping, and shipping the wrong goods normally is both an acceptance and a breach. The exception is when the seller seasonably tells the buyer the shipment is only an accommodation. Then it is a counteroffer that the buyer is free to turn down.","id":"b14"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer and a seller clearly intend to make a binding contract for 200 bags of mulch, but they never mention a price. Under the UCC, what is the result?","o":["There is a contract, and the price is a reasonable price at the time for delivery.","There is no contract, because price is an essential term.","There is a contract, and the seller may name any price it wishes.","There is a contract, and the price is whatever the buyer last paid anyone for mulch."],"a":0,"w":"Under UCC section 2-204, a contract for goods does not fail for indefiniteness if the parties intended to make a deal and there is a reasonable basis for a remedy. Section 2-305 then fills the open price term with a reasonable price at the time for delivery.","id":"b15"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"After 35 years of service, a bookkeeper is told by her company's board: \"In gratitude, we will pay you $800 a month for life whenever you decide to retire. You are free to keep working as long as you like.\" She retires a year later, at an age when finding new work is unrealistic. The company pays for two years and then stops. What is her best argument?","o":["Her 35 years of past work were consideration for the promise.","Her retirement was bargained-for consideration, because the company demanded it.","Promissory estoppel, because she relied on the promise by retiring, as the company should have expected.","She has no argument, because the promise was a gift."],"a":2,"w":"Past work is not consideration, and the company did not ask her to retire in exchange for the pension. But under Restatement section 90, a promise that foreseeably induces real reliance is binding if injustice can be avoided only by enforcing it. Giving up a job in reliance on a pension is the classic example.","id":"b16"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"A general contractor preparing a bid for a new library receives a bid from a plumbing subcontractor of $90,000. The general uses that figure in its own bid, as the plumber expected, and wins the job. Before the general can formally accept, the plumber calls to say the bid was a mistake and is withdrawn. Under the modern approach, what is the result?","o":["The plumber is held to its bid, because the general's foreseeable reliance made the offer irrevocable for a reasonable time.","The plumber may withdraw, because any offer can be revoked before acceptance.","The plumber may withdraw, because bids are never offers.","The plumber is held to its bid only if the general paid for an option."],"a":0,"w":"This is the rule of Drennan v. Star Paving and Restatement section 87. When an offeror should expect the offeree to rely substantially before accepting, and the offeree does, the offer becomes binding like an option to the extent needed to avoid injustice. The general must still accept promptly and may not shop the bid around.","id":"b17"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"Nell checks her coat at a theatre and is handed a small numbered stub. She puts it in her pocket without looking at it. On the back, in tiny print, it says the theatre's liability for lost items is limited to $10. Nothing else tells customers about the limit. Her $600 coat is lost. Is she bound by the limit?","o":["Yes, because everyone is bound by whatever is printed on a paper they accept.","Probably not, because a reasonable person would see the stub as a mere claim token and not as a document containing contract terms.","Yes, because limits on liability are always enforceable.","Probably not, because theatres are not allowed to use form contracts."],"a":1,"w":"The duty to read applies to documents that a reasonable person would understand to contain contract terms. A coat check stub looks like a simple receipt for identifying property. Without reasonable notice that it carried terms, Nell did not assent to the limit.","id":"b18"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"A ride app's sign-up screen has a large \"Register\" button. Directly beneath it, on an uncluttered screen, clear text reads, \"By creating an account you agree to the Terms of Service,\" with the last three words shown as a bright underlined link. There is no box to tick. Which is the most accurate statement?","o":["The terms cannot bind anyone, because there was no checkbox.","The terms bind only users who actually opened the link.","This is browsewrap, which is never enforceable.","The terms are likely enforceable, because the notice was reasonably conspicuous and pressing the button showed assent."],"a":3,"w":"This design is often called sign-in wrap. Courts, as in Meyer v. Uber, ask whether a reasonably prudent user would have noticed that terms applied and whether the user then took an action that plainly signals agreement. A clear notice right beside the button usually satisfies both.","id":"b19"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"Hal orders a computer by phone. It arrives in a box with a sheet of terms, including an arbitration clause, stating that the terms apply unless the computer is returned within 30 days. Hal keeps the computer for three months and then tries to sue in court. Under the approach of Hill v. Gateway, what is the result?","o":["Hal is not bound, because the contract was complete when he paid by phone.","Hal is not bound, because he never signed the sheet.","Hal is bound, because he had a chance to read the terms and accepted them by keeping the computer past the return period.","Hal is bound only if he read the sheet from start to finish."],"a":2,"w":"Under the rolling contract approach of ProCD and Hill v. Gateway, the seller can propose that the deal is formed after the buyer has had a chance to inspect the terms. Keeping the product past the return period is the acceptance. Not every court agrees, but that is the rule those cases stand for.","id":"b20"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer sends a purchase order for steel. The seller's reply says, \"Our acceptance is expressly conditional on your assent to the terms on this form.\" The buyer never assents. The seller ships anyway, and the buyer takes and pays for the steel. A dispute arises. What are the terms of the contract?","o":["The terms on which the two forms agree, plus the UCC's gap-filling rules.","The seller's terms, because its form was sent last.","The buyer's terms, because its form was sent first.","There is no contract at all, because the forms never matched."],"a":0,"w":"The seller's conditional reply was not an acceptance, so the forms alone made no contract. But under UCC section 2-207, subsection 3, conduct by both sides that recognizes a contract is enough to create one. Its terms are those on which the writings agree, together with the UCC's default provisions.","id":"b21"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A consumer mails an order for a grill. The seller's acceptance form adds a new term requiring all disputes to be heard in a distant state. The consumer never responds to that term. Which is the most accurate statement about the added term?","o":["It automatically becomes part of the contract unless the consumer objects.","It is only a proposal, and it does not become part of the contract unless the consumer agrees to it.","It prevents any contract from forming.","It replaces every term in the consumer's order."],"a":1,"w":"Under UCC section 2-207, subsection 2, additional terms in an acceptance are treated as proposals for addition to the contract. They can slip in automatically only when both parties are merchants. With a consumer on one side, the new term is in only if the consumer actually agrees.","id":"b22"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"both","q":"When sources of meaning conflict, courts rank them. From the most weight to the least, which order is correct?","o":["Usage of trade, course of dealing, course of performance, express terms.","Course of dealing, express terms, usage of trade, course of performance.","Express terms, course of performance, course of dealing, usage of trade.","Express terms, usage of trade, course of dealing, course of performance."],"a":2,"w":"Under UCC section 1-303 and Restatement section 203, express terms come first. Next is course of performance, which is how the parties have acted under this very contract. Then comes course of dealing from their earlier contracts, and last is usage of trade in the industry.","id":"b23"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A buyer agrees to purchase crates of tea \"arriving on the freighter Dolphin.\" Unknown to both sides, two freighters are named Dolphin. One docks in May and the other in September. The buyer honestly meant the May ship, and the seller honestly meant the September ship. Neither had any reason to know of the other's meaning. What is the result?","o":["A contract on the buyer's meaning, because the buyer is paying.","A contract on the seller's meaning, because the seller owns the tea.","A contract for delivery halfway between the two dates.","No contract, because there was no mutual assent to the same thing."],"a":3,"w":"This is the rule of Raffles v. Wichelhaus and Restatement section 20. When the parties attach materially different meanings to a term, and neither knows or has reason to know what the other means, there is no meeting of the minds and no contract. If one side had reason to know, the innocent side's meaning would control.","id":"b24"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A court finds that a written contract is the final statement of the terms it contains, but not a complete statement of the whole deal. Which evidence of an earlier oral agreement may be admitted?","o":["None, because any final writing excludes all earlier agreements.","Evidence of a consistent additional term that does not contradict the writing.","Evidence of a term that contradicts the writing, so long as the witness is credible.","Any evidence at all, because the writing is incomplete."],"a":1,"w":"A writing that is final but incomplete is a partial integration. Under Restatement sections 213 and 216, it cannot be contradicted by earlier agreements, but it can be supplemented by consistent additional terms. Only a complete integration shuts out consistent additions as well.","id":"b25"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"On March 1, a landscaper and a homeowner sign a fully integrated written contract for a $4,000 patio. On March 20, they agree orally to add a fire pit for another $600. The homeowner later argues that the parol evidence rule bars any testimony about the fire pit agreement. Is the homeowner right?","o":["No, because the rule applies only to agreements made before or at the time of the writing, not to later ones.","Yes, because the writing was fully integrated.","Yes, because oral agreements are never enforceable.","No, because the rule applies only to sales of goods."],"a":0,"w":"The parol evidence rule protects a final writing from earlier or contemporaneous side agreements. It has nothing to say about agreements made afterward. A later modification may raise other issues, such as consideration or a clause requiring written changes, but the parol evidence rule is not one of them.","id":"b26"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"Two merchants agree by phone on a sale of $8,000 of coffee beans. The next day the seller sends the buyer a signed written confirmation stating the quantity. The buyer reads it, files it, and says nothing. A month later the buyer denies there was a deal and raises the statute of frauds. What is the result?","o":["The defence succeeds, because the buyer never signed anything.","The defence succeeds, because phone deals for goods are void.","The defence fails only if the buyer admits the deal in court.","The defence fails, because a merchant who receives a written confirmation and does not object within ten days loses the defence."],"a":3,"w":"Under UCC section 2-201, subsection 2, a written confirmation between merchants that would bind the sender also satisfies the statute against the recipient. The recipient must have reason to know its contents and must fail to object in writing within ten days. The seller still has to prove the oral deal was actually made.","id":"b27"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"On January 2, a zoo orally hires Vic to work as a keeper for exactly one year, starting on March 1. In June the zoo fires him without cause and raises the statute of frauds. What is the result?","o":["The statute does not apply, because the job lasts only one year.","The statute does not apply, because Vic had already started work.","The statute applies, because the contract could not be fully performed within one year from the day it was made.","The statute applies, because every employment contract must be in writing."],"a":2,"w":"The one-year provision, found in Restatement section 130, covers contracts that cannot be fully performed within a year of their making. The year is measured from the date of the agreement and not from the start of work. Performance here would end fourteen months after January 2, so a signed writing is required.","id":"b28"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"A shop owner signs a ten-year equipment lease at a rate that is fair on the day of signing. Five years later, prices for that equipment collapse, and the old rate now looks painfully high. The owner asks a court to strike the lease as unconscionable. What is the most accurate statement?","o":["The lease is unconscionable, because it is harsh today.","The claim fails, because unconscionability is judged as of the time the contract was made.","The lease is unconscionable, because long leases are inherently unfair.","The claim fails, because businesses may never raise unconscionability."],"a":1,"w":"Under UCC section 2-302 and Restatement section 208, the question is whether the contract or term was unconscionable at the time it was made. A deal that was fair at signing does not become unconscionable because the market later moved. Changed circumstances belong to other doctrines, such as impracticability.","id":"b29"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A buyer purchases a rental cottage. Both parties believe its septic system works. The signed contract says the buyer has inspected the property and accepts it \"as is, in its present condition.\" The system turns out to be hopelessly defective, and the cottage is condemned. The buyer seeks rescission for mutual mistake. What is the likely result?","o":["Rescission is denied, because the as-is clause placed the risk of the mistake on the buyer.","Rescission is granted, because the mistake was mutual and material.","Rescission is granted, because sellers always bear the risk of defects.","Rescission is denied, because mistake never applies to land."],"a":0,"w":"Mutual mistake gives no relief to a party who bears the risk of the mistake. Under Restatement section 154, a party bears the risk when the agreement assigns it to him. As in Lenawee County v. Messerly, an as-is clause does exactly that.","id":"b30"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT required to avoid a contract for mutual mistake under the Restatement?","o":["Both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption on which the contract was made.","The mistake had a material effect on the agreed exchange.","The other party caused the mistake by making a false statement.","The party seeking relief did not bear the risk of the mistake."],"a":2,"w":"Restatement section 152 asks for a shared mistake about a basic assumption, a material effect on the exchange, and a party who does not bear the risk. Nobody has to be at fault. If one side caused the error by a false statement, that would be misrepresentation, which is a separate defence.","id":"b31"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A used-truck dealer tells a buyer, \"This truck got a brand-new transmission last month.\" The dealer never says \"warranty\" or \"guarantee.\" The buyer buys the truck, and the transmission turns out to be the worn-out original. Which is the most accurate statement?","o":["There is no warranty, because the dealer never used the word \"warranty.\"","There is no warranty, because the statement was not in writing.","There is no warranty, because statements about used goods are always opinion.","The dealer made an express warranty, because it stated a fact about the goods that became part of the basis of the bargain."],"a":3,"w":"Under UCC section 2-313, any affirmation of fact or promise about the goods that becomes part of the basis of the bargain creates an express warranty. No special words or intention are needed. A specific claim about a new transmission is a fact, not mere sales talk.","id":"b32"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"Ivy tells a hardware store clerk, \"I need paint for the inside of my hot tub. You pick, since I know nothing about paint.\" The clerk hands her a can of perfectly good interior wall paint. It peels off within a week of hot water. Which warranty gives Ivy her strongest claim?","o":["The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose.","The implied warranty of merchantability.","The warranty of title.","No warranty, because the clerk never promised anything."],"a":0,"w":"Under UCC section 2-315, a fitness warranty arises when the seller has reason to know the buyer's particular purpose and that the buyer is relying on the seller's skill to choose suitable goods. The paint was fine for ordinary walls, so merchantability is the weaker claim. It simply was not fit for the special use Ivy described.","id":"b33"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Before showing his house, a seller paints over large water stains on the basement ceiling so buyers will not suspect flooding. He says nothing at all about water. A buyer inspects, sees a clean ceiling, and buys. The basement floods in the first storm. Can the buyer avoid the contract?","o":["No, because the seller never made any statement.","Yes, because actively hiding a fact is treated the same as asserting that the fact does not exist.","No, because the buyer had a chance to inspect.","Yes, but only if the contract included a written warranty against flooding."],"a":1,"w":"Under Restatement section 160, an action intended to keep the other party from learning a fact is equivalent to an assertion that the fact does not exist. Painting over the stains was a misrepresentation made by conduct. It was fraudulent and the buyer justifiably relied on it, so the contract is voidable.","id":"b34"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Under the Restatement, which misrepresentation makes a contract voidable by a party who justifiably relied on it?","o":["Only a misrepresentation that was both fraudulent and material.","Only a misrepresentation made in writing.","Only a misrepresentation the speaker knew to be false.","A misrepresentation that was either fraudulent or material."],"a":3,"w":"Under Restatement section 164, a contract is voidable if assent was induced by a misrepresentation that is either fraudulent or material, and the recipient was justified in relying on it. So an honest but important false statement can still undo the deal. Section 162 defines what makes a misrepresentation fraudulent or material.","id":"b35"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A wedding couple hires a well-known harpist to play at their ceremony. Two days before the wedding, the harpist breaks both wrists in a bicycle accident that was not her fault. The couple sues her for breach. What is the result?","o":["She is liable, because she promised to play.","She is liable unless she sends a substitute harpist.","She is excused, because her ability to perform personally was a basic assumption of the contract.","She is excused only if the contract had a clause about injuries."],"a":2,"w":"Under Restatement section 262, when a particular person is necessary to perform a duty, that person's death or incapacity makes performance impracticable. The general rule of section 261 then discharges the duty, because the event was not her fault and its non-occurrence was a basic assumption of the deal.","id":"b36"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A tenant signs a three-year lease of a storefront, planning to sell fireworks. The lease allows \"any lawful retail use.\" A year later the city bans the sale of fireworks. The tenant could still sell other merchandise there, though less profitably. The tenant stops paying rent and claims frustration of purpose. What is the likely result?","o":["The tenant is excused, because its plan for the store is now illegal.","The tenant is not excused, because the purpose of the lease was not substantially frustrated when other retail uses remain.","The tenant is excused, because the lease became less profitable.","The tenant is not excused, because frustration never applies to leases."],"a":1,"w":"Under Restatement section 265, frustration requires that a party's principal purpose be substantially frustrated by an event whose non-occurrence was a basic assumption of the contract. A lease that still allows other valuable uses has not lost its point. A deal that has merely become less profitable is not frustrated.","id":"b37"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Under the Restatement, when is a contract voidable because of a party's mental illness or defect?","o":["When the person could not understand the transaction, or could not act reasonably in relation to it and the other party had reason to know of the condition.","Whenever the person has ever been diagnosed with any mental illness.","Only when a court had already appointed a guardian before the contract was signed.","Only when the other party deliberately took advantage of the person."],"a":0,"w":"Restatement section 15 has two tests. The first asks whether the person was unable to understand the nature and consequences of the transaction. The second asks whether the person was unable to act reasonably and the other party had reason to know it. A diagnosis alone is not enough.","id":"b38"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A shop owner discovers that an employee's adult son, who also works there, has been taking money from the till. The owner tells the employee, \"Sign this note promising to repay $20,000, or I go to the police and your son goes to jail.\" Terrified, she signs. The son really was guilty. Can she avoid the note?","o":["No, because the son really did take the money.","No, because reporting a crime is always lawful.","Yes, because a threat of criminal prosecution is an improper threat, and she had no reasonable alternative.","Yes, but only if the threat was made in writing."],"a":2,"w":"Under Restatement section 176, a threat of criminal prosecution is improper when it is used to extract a contract, even if the accused person is guilty. Under section 175, a contract induced by an improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative is voidable. The owner may go to the police, but may not trade silence for a signature.","id":"b39"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Which statement about undue influence is most accurate?","o":["It requires a threat of physical or economic harm.","It requires a false statement of fact.","It makes the contract void, so neither side can ever enforce it.","It involves unfair persuasion of a person who is under the persuader's domination or who reasonably trusts the persuader to act in that person's interest."],"a":3,"w":"Under Restatement section 177, undue influence is unfair persuasion of someone who is dominated by the persuader or who is justified, because of their relationship, in assuming the persuader will look out for them. No threat and no lie are needed. The result is a contract that is voidable by the victim.","id":"b40"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"An owner hires a builder to add a sunroom for $100,000. After the owner has paid $60,000 in progress payments, the builder walks off the job without excuse. The owner reasonably hires a second builder, who charges $55,000 to finish the same sunroom. What are the owner's expectation damages?","o":["$55,000","$15,000","$40,000","$5,000"],"a":1,"w":"Expectation damages give the owner the benefit of the bargain, which was a finished sunroom for $100,000. The owner ended up paying $60,000 plus $55,000, or $115,000. The extra $15,000 is the cost of completion minus the $40,000 the owner still owed the first builder.","id":"b41"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A surgeon promises a patient that an operation will give him \"a one hundred percent perfect hand.\" The operation leaves the hand worse than before. On a breach of contract claim, what is the expectation measure of damages?","o":["The difference between the value of the perfect hand that was promised and the value of the hand as it is after the operation.","The difference between the value of the hand before the operation and its value afterward.","The patient's pain and suffering from the operation.","A refund of the surgeon's fee and nothing more."],"a":0,"w":"This is Hawkins v. McGee. Expectation damages put the injured party where full performance would have put him, so the measure is the value promised minus the value received. Comparing the hand before and after would be a reliance measure, which only restores the starting position.","id":"b42"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A restaurant wrongfully fires its head chef six months into a one-year contract. The chef turns down the only job she can find, washing dishes at a diner for a fraction of the pay, and stays unemployed. The restaurant argues that her damages must be reduced by what she would have earned washing dishes. What is the result?","o":["Damages are reduced, because any job counts as mitigation.","Damages are eliminated, because she refused work.","Damages are reduced only if the diner was nearby.","Damages are not reduced, because she need only accept work that is comparable and not different or inferior."],"a":3,"w":"The duty to mitigate requires only reasonable efforts. As in Parker v. Twentieth Century-Fox, a wrongfully discharged employee does not have to take a job of a different or inferior kind. Only earnings from substantially similar work that she could have found are subtracted.","id":"b43"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A supplier deliberately breaks a contract because a competitor of the buyer offered a higher price. The breach is not also a tort. The buyer asks for punitive damages to teach the supplier a lesson. What is the general rule?","o":["Punitive damages are available for any deliberate breach.","Punitive damages are available whenever the breach was profitable.","Punitive damages are not recoverable for breach of contract unless the conduct is also a tort for which they are allowed.","Punitive damages replace expectation damages when a breach is in bad faith."],"a":2,"w":"Under Restatement section 355, punitive damages are not recoverable for a breach of contract unless the conduct is also a tort that supports them. Contract remedies aim to compensate the injured party, not to punish the one who breached. The buyer is limited to its actual, provable loss.","id":"b44"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A famous singer signs a contract to perform only at the Starlight Lounge for three months. After two weeks she quits to sing at a rival club across the street for more money. The Starlight asks a court for help. What is the court most likely to do?","o":["Refuse to order her to sing at the Starlight, but possibly enjoin her from singing at the rival club during the contract term.","Order her to sing at the Starlight, because her talent is unique.","Order the rival club to hand over its ticket sales.","Do nothing, because courts never grant any relief in personal service contracts."],"a":0,"w":"Under Restatement section 367, a promise to render personal service will not be specifically enforced. But as in Lumley v. Wagner, a court may issue a negative injunction that stops a performer with unique talents from working for a competitor during the contract period.","id":"b45"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A builder finishes a house for $300,000. The work complies with the plans except for a few minor defects in the trim that would cost $1,200 to fix. The owner, who still owes the final $80,000 payment, refuses to pay any of it because the performance was \"not perfect.\" What is the result?","o":["The owner owes nothing until every defect is corrected.","The owner owes the $80,000 balance, minus damages for the minor defects.","The owner owes the full $80,000 with no deduction.","The owner may cancel the contract and keep the house for the amount already paid."],"a":1,"w":"Under the common law doctrine of substantial performance, a party who has performed in all important respects may recover the contract price. Only a material breach excuses the other side from paying. The owner's remedy for trivial defects is a deduction for damages, here about $1,200.","id":"b46"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A bakery contracts for twelve monthly deliveries of flour. The third delivery is slightly short, and the seller offers at once to make up the shortage. The bakery, which has found a cheaper supplier, announces that it is cancelling the whole contract. Is the bakery entitled to cancel?","o":["Yes, because under perfect tender any defect allows the buyer to cancel everything.","Yes, because a buyer may always cancel after a late or short delivery.","No, because in an installment contract the buyer may cancel the whole only if the defect substantially impairs the value of the whole contract.","No, because installment contracts can never be cancelled."],"a":2,"w":"The perfect tender rule of UCC section 2-601 does not govern installment contracts. Under section 2-612, a buyer may reject an installment only if the defect substantially impairs its value and cannot be cured. The buyer may cancel the whole contract only if the defect substantially impairs the value of the whole.","id":"b47"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer agrees to purchase a custom sign for $10,000 and then wrongfully refuses to take it. The seller gives proper notice and resells the sign in a commercially reasonable sale for $7,000, spending $200 on transporting it to the new buyer. The seller saved no expenses because of the breach. What can the seller recover?","o":["$3,000","$10,000","$7,200","$3,200"],"a":3,"w":"Under UCC section 2-706, a seller who resells in good faith and in a commercially reasonable manner recovers the contract price minus the resale price, plus incidental damages, less any expenses saved. That is $10,000 minus $7,000, plus $200, for a total of $3,200.","id":"b48"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A city hires a contractor to build a new sports arena. Which person is only an incidental beneficiary, with no right to sue the contractor if the arena is never built?","o":["The owner of a nearby diner who expected extra customers from arena crowds.","A bank that the contract expressly says the contractor will pay in order to discharge a debt the city owes it.","A team that the contract names and says the arena is being built for, with a right to enforce the completion date.","The city itself."],"a":0,"w":"Under Restatement section 302, only an intended beneficiary may enforce a contract. That means someone the parties meant to give the benefit of the promised performance. A person who merely happens to gain from the contract, like the diner owner, is an incidental beneficiary and under section 315 has no rights.","id":"b49"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"both","q":"A homeowner hires Pipes Plumbing to replace a water heater, an ordinary job. Pipes is busy and delegates the work to Drips Plumbing, a competent firm. The homeowner never agrees to release Pipes. Drips installs the heater badly, and it floods the basement. Who is liable to the homeowner for the breach?","o":["Only Drips, because Pipes handed over the duty.","Pipes remains liable, because delegating a duty does not release the original party unless the other side agrees to a substitution.","No one, because the delegation made the contract void.","Only Drips, because the homeowner accepted its work."],"a":1,"w":"A duty that does not depend on personal skill may be delegated, but delegation does not free the delegating party. Under Restatement section 318 and UCC section 2-210, the original party stays liable unless the other side agrees to a novation that substitutes the new party. Pipes still answers for the bad work.","id":"b50"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"Dot signs a one-page contract with Splash Brothers for a backyard koi pond. The invoice lists $2,000 for the liner, pump and rocks, and $9,000 for design, digging and labour. Dot pays a $1,000 deposit. The finished pond leaks slowly because the digging was sloppy. Dot announces that the pond \"is not perfect\" and that she may therefore reject the whole thing and pay nothing more. The brothers reply that the pond is \"basically fine.\" Which issue must a student resolve before anything else?","o":["Whether the brothers made a firm offer","Whether the perfect tender rule lets Dot reject the pond","Whether Article 2 or the common law governs a mixed deal for goods and services","Whether Dot's deposit counts as consideration"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is one contract that bundles movable things, the liner, pump and rocks, with a much larger amount of labour. UCC section 2-105 covers goods, so for a mixed deal most courts ask what the predominant purpose was, and that choice decides everything that follows. Perfect tender is tempting because Dot says the pond is not perfect, but you cannot know whether perfect tender or substantial performance applies until you have picked the rulebook.","id":"i1"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"While Mrs. Okoro is away on holiday, her neighbour Zed notices her fence has blown down. Without being asked, he spends a weekend and $80 of his own lumber rebuilding it. When she returns she is delighted and writes on a sticky note, \"For fixing my fence I will pay you $300 on Friday. Signed, Mrs. O.\" On Friday she changes her mind and offers him a fruit basket. Zed sues for the $300. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether a promise made after the work was already done is supported by consideration","Promissory estoppel","The statute of frauds","Economic duress"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is the order of events: Zed finished the fence before Mrs. Okoro promised anything. Under Restatement section 71, consideration must be bargained for, meaning the promise and the performance are each given in exchange for the other, and a past favour was not given in exchange for a later promise. Promissory estoppel is tempting because Zed spent $80, but he spent it before the promise, so he did not rely on the promise at all.","id":"i2"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Gil mows lawns in the summer. He texts his neighbour Wanda, \"Thinking about maybe taking on one more lawn this year. I would want at least $50 a week.\" Wanda texts back within a minute, \"Accepted! See you Saturday,\" and that afternoon she buys a new sprinkler to celebrate. Gil never shows up and says he decided to take up fishing. Wanda insists they have a contract. A student writing this exam answer should start with which issue?","o":["Promissory estoppel","The mailbox rule","Whether Wanda's acceptance mirrored the terms of the offer","Whether Gil's text was an offer or only an invitation to negotiate"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is Gil's hedged language: thinking about, maybe, and at least. Under Restatement section 24, an offer is a manifestation of willingness to enter a bargain that justifies the other person in believing her assent will conclude it, and floating a minimum price usually does not do that. Promissory estoppel is tempting because of the sprinkler, but Gil made no promise for Wanda to rely on, so the first question is whether there was an offer at all.","id":"i3"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A bakery owner hands Raj a written job offer for an at-will position. It says, \"To accept, sign this letter and hand it to me in person at the bakery by 5 p.m. Friday. No other form of acceptance will do.\" On Thursday Raj leaves her a cheerful voicemail saying \"I accept!\" and turns down a job at a rival doughnut shop. On Friday at 6 p.m. he slides the signed letter under the bakery's locked door. On Saturday the owner hires her cousin instead. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Promissory estoppel","Whether Raj accepted in the manner and within the time the offer required","The statute of frauds","Whether the offer was too indefinite to be accepted"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is that the offer spells out exactly how and when to accept and says nothing else will do. Under Restatement section 60, when an offer prescribes the manner of acceptance, its terms must be complied with, and under section 36 an offer lapses at the time it sets. Promissory estoppel is tempting because Raj turned down another job, but the owner only made an offer with conditions, so you start by asking whether a voicemail and a late letter could accept it.","id":"i4"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A farmer and a grocer swap friendly emails saying, \"We should do pumpkins again this autumn.\" Nobody mentions a price and nothing formal is signed. In October the farmer delivers 300 pumpkins. The grocer stacks them by the front door and sells half of them, although a few are a bit lumpy. When the farmer sends a bill, the grocer refuses to pay, saying, \"We never agreed on a price, so there was never a contract.\" Which issue does the grocer's argument raise?","o":["Whether conduct by both sides can form a sale contract even though the price was left open","Whether the pumpkins breached the implied warranty of merchantability","The battle of the forms","Whether the farmer made a firm offer"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is that both sides behaved as if they had a deal: one delivered and the other displayed and sold the goods. Under UCC section 2-204, a contract for sale can be made in any manner that shows agreement, including conduct, and it does not fail just because a term such as price is left open. The lumpy pumpkins tempt you toward warranty, but the grocer is not complaining about quality; he is denying that any contract ever formed.","id":"i5"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"Tile Barn, a tile wholesaler, emails a contractor a quotation signed by its sales manager: \"2,000 slate tiles at $3 each. This price will be held open for you until the end of the month.\" The contractor pays nothing for this promise and does nothing with it for two weeks. Slate prices then jump, and Tile Barn emails, \"Quote withdrawn.\" The next day, still within the month, the contractor replies, \"I accept your quote.\" Tile Barn says it was free to pull the offer. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["The mailbox rule","Promissory estoppel","Whether a merchant's signed promise to hold an offer open is irrevocable without consideration","The merchant confirmation exception to the statute of frauds"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is a merchant, a signed writing, and the words held open until the end of the month. Under UCC section 2-205, a merchant's signed written assurance that an offer to buy or sell goods will be held open makes it irrevocable for the stated time, up to three months, even with no consideration. Promissory estoppel is tempting, but the contractor did nothing in reliance for two weeks, so the firm offer rule is the doctrine doing the work.","id":"i6"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"Pia runs a small bakery. A sandwich chain's regional agent tells her several times, \"Sell your bakery and move to Elm City, and a franchise is yours for $18,000.\" The lease, the royalty rate and the territory are never discussed, and no contract is ever signed. Pia sells her bakery at a loss, moves her family and her very large cat to Elm City, and rents a flat. The chain then says it has changed its mind. Pia wants to recover what the move cost her. Which doctrine should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the franchise agreement satisfies the statute of frauds","Unilateral mistake","Whether the agent made an offer that Pia accepted by moving","Whether Pia's reliance on the agent's assurances makes them enforceable without a bargain"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is a repeated promise, no finished contract, and a promisee who gave up a great deal because of what she was told. Under Restatement section 90, a promise that the promisor should reasonably expect to induce action, and that does induce it, is binding if injustice can be avoided only by enforcement. Offer and acceptance is tempting, but the key terms were never settled, so there is no bargain to enforce and reliance is Pia's route.","id":"i7"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A brewery sends a bottle maker its standard purchase order for 20,000 amber bottles. The bottle maker replies with its own printed acknowledgment, which matches the order on price, quantity and delivery date, but adds on the back, \"All disputes will be arbitrated in the seller's home city.\" Nobody reads either form. The bottles arrive, the brewery fills them, and later some of them crack. The brewery sues in court and the bottle maker demands arbitration. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether the acknowledgment was a counter-offer under the mirror image rule","Whether an additional term in the seller's acknowledgment became part of the contract","Whether the arbitration clause is unconscionable","The parol evidence rule"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is two merchants trading printed forms that agree on the main terms but differ on a detail in the small print. For goods, UCC section 2-207 says a definite acceptance forms a contract even though it adds terms, and it then asks whether the additional term became part of the deal or dropped out, for example because it materially alters the contract. The mirror image rule is tempting, but that is the common law approach that section 2-207 replaces for sales of goods.","id":"i8"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"Bo and Fay hire a wedding band called The Soggy Biscuits. The signed contract says the band will play \"three sets\" for $1,500, paid in advance. The band plays three sets of twenty minutes each and goes home at nine o'clock. Bo and Fay are furious, because they understood a set to mean forty-five minutes, which is how every other wedding band in town uses the word. The band says that to them a set has always been twenty minutes. The drummer also arrived ten minutes late. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Mutual mistake about a basic assumption","Whether the contract is too indefinite to enforce","Whose meaning of an ambiguous contract term controls","Liquidated damages"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is one written word, sets, that each side honestly reads differently, plus a local trade usage about what it means. Under Restatement sections 201 and 202, a court asks whose meaning should prevail and uses tools such as usage of trade and the circumstances to decide. Mutual mistake is tempting, but the parties are not wrong about a fact in the world; they disagree about what their own words mean, and that is an interpretation problem.","id":"i9"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Juno rents a music studio from a landlord under a signed five-page lease. The lease describes the studio, the rent and the term, and ends with, \"This document is the entire agreement between the parties.\" It says nothing about the roof. Juno insists that the week before signing, the landlord shook her hand and said she could use the rooftop for free concerts. The landlord, who honestly does not remember saying it, has now padlocked the roof door. Juno wants to testify about the handshake promise. A student writing this exam answer should start with which issue?","o":["Whether the parol evidence rule bars proof of the earlier oral promise","Whether the rooftop promise fails for lack of consideration","Fraudulent misrepresentation","Whether Juno's interpretation of the word studio controls"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is a signed writing that calls itself the entire agreement, and a party who wants to add an oral promise made before it was signed. Under Restatement sections 209, 210 and 213, a completely integrated writing discharges earlier agreements within its scope, so the first question is whether the lease is integrated and whether the rooftop promise can come in at all. Fraud is tempting, but the facts say the landlord was honest, so the fraud exception has nothing to work with.","id":"i10"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"At a crowded barbecue, Lou, a retired dentist, agrees out loud to sell his vintage pinball machine to Bea for $1,200. They shake hands in front of a dozen guests and agree that Bea will collect it and pay on Sunday. Nothing is written down, no money changes hands and the machine stays in Lou's garage. On Saturday a collector offers Lou $2,000, and Lou tells Bea the deal is off. Bea points out that twelve people heard him. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up for Lou?","o":["Lack of consideration","The parol evidence rule","Revocation of a firm offer","The statute of frauds for a sale of goods priced at $500 or more"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is an oral deal for goods at $1,200 with no writing, no payment and no delivery. Under UCC section 2-201, a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more is not enforceable without a writing signed by the party to be charged, unless an exception such as part payment or accepted goods applies, and none does here. The parol evidence rule is tempting because both involve writings, but that rule is about adding to a writing that exists, and here there is no writing at all.","id":"i11"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"Marge buys a new blender from an appliance shop. The shop window has a banner reading \"Best prices in town!\" The clerk rings up the sale without saying a word about the blender, and Marge does not mention what she plans to make. At home, the blender cannot get through a ripe banana and the motor smokes. The shop refuses a refund, saying, \"We never promised you anything about it.\" Marge simply wanted a blender that blends. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Express warranty based on the banner","The implied warranty of merchantability","The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a merchant that sells appliances, goods that fail at their ordinary job, and no statements about the goods by anyone. Under UCC section 2-314, a merchant who deals in goods of the kind automatically warrants that they are fit for the ordinary purposes for which such goods are used, so the shop's silence does not help it. Fitness for a particular purpose is tempting, but under section 2-315 that needs a special purpose the seller knew about, and blending a banana is as ordinary as it gets.","id":"i12"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"On moving day, a moving company's foreman hands Oleg a pre-printed service contract in his driveway while the truck idles. He says, \"Standard form, sign at the bottom,\" and says nothing else. The terms cannot be changed. In pale grey type on the back, the form says the customer gives up every claim for damaged belongings, that the company may raise its price at any time, and that a customer who sues must pay three times the company's legal fees. Oleg signs without turning the page. The movers later drop his piano. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up for Oleg against those clauses?","o":["Unconscionability","Economic duress","Unilateral mistake","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is the pairing of an unfair process, a take-it-or-leave-it form with harsh clauses hidden in pale type, and unfairly one-sided terms. Under Restatement section 208, a court may refuse to enforce a term that was unconscionable when the contract was made, and courts usually look for both procedural and substantive unfairness. Duress is tempting because Oleg was hurried, but nobody made an improper threat; being rushed through a form is a bargaining process problem, which is the home ground of unconscionability.","id":"i13"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A driver bumps a cyclist named Kit. A clinic takes an X-ray and tells them both that Kit has nothing worse than a bruised knee. Relying on that report, Kit and the driver sign a settlement: the driver pays $800 and Kit gives up his claim. Kit signs quickly because he wants the money for a new bicycle bell. A month later a specialist finds that the knee ligament was torn all along and needs a $40,000 operation. The driver never said anything untrue. Which doctrine should Kit's lawyer analyse first?","o":["Unconscionability","Fraudulent misrepresentation","Mutual mistake about a basic assumption of the contract","Lack of consideration for the release"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is that both parties shared the same wrong belief, from the same clinic report, about a fact that existed when they signed. Under Restatement section 152, a contract is voidable when both parties are mistaken about a basic assumption, the mistake materially affects the exchange, and the party seeking relief does not bear the risk under section 154. Misrepresentation is tempting because someone gave bad information, but it was the clinic, not the driver, and the driver believed it too.","id":"i14"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Tess is viewing a flat and asks the landlord directly, \"Has this place ever had mice?\" The landlord, who has paid an exterminator to visit every month for two years, smiles and says, \"Never. Not one.\" The rent is unusually low and Tess does not look inside the cupboards. She signs a one-year lease. On her first night she meets a large and confident family of mice. Tess wants out of the lease. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up?","o":["Unilateral mistake","Fraudulent misrepresentation of a material fact","Unconscionability","Frustration of purpose"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a direct question, a flatly false answer, and a speaker who knew the truth. Under Restatement sections 162 and 164, a contract is voidable when assent is induced by a fraudulent or material misrepresentation on which the other party was justified in relying. Unilateral mistake is tempting because Tess was wrong about the mice, but her mistake was caused by the landlord's lie, and misrepresentation is the more direct and much easier route.","id":"i15"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Mr. Pym, aged 60, is in the middle of a manic episode that his doctor has documented. He arrives at a builder's yard wearing three hats, talks at length to a hedge, and signs a contract for a $90,000 replica pirate ship in his garden. The builder remarks to a colleague that the customer \"seems seriously unwell\" but takes the deposit anyway. The price is fair for a pirate ship, and Mr. Pym read the whole contract aloud before signing. The builder did nothing to push him. A week later his family wants the contract cancelled. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Undue influence","Unconscionability","Economic duress","Whether mental illness made the contract voidable because the builder had reason to know of his condition"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is a documented mental illness, wildly out-of-character behaviour, and a builder who noticed something was wrong. Under Restatement section 15, a contract is voidable if a person cannot understand the transaction, or cannot act reasonably about it and the other party has reason to know of the condition. Undue influence is tempting, but the builder did not persuade or pressure him at all; the problem lies in Mr. Pym's own condition, which is a capacity question.","id":"i16"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"Ned and Vera are partners in a noodle stall. Vera has a video of Ned singing very badly at an office party. She tells him, \"Sign your half of the stall over to me for $1,000 tonight, or this goes to your new employer and your mother-in-law.\" Ned's half is worth about $30,000. Vera tells no lies about the stall or its value. Ned, mortified and seeing no other way out, signs. He now wants the transfer undone. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up?","o":["Duress by improper threat","Undue influence","Unconscionability","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is the phrase sign or else: Vera threatens to harm Ned in a way that gains her nothing except his signature. Under Restatement sections 175 and 176, a contract is voidable if assent is induced by an improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative, and a threat is improper when the exchange is unfair and the threatened act would harm the victim without really benefiting the person making it. Undue influence is tempting because they are partners, but that doctrine is about unfair persuasion by someone you trust, and this is a threat.","id":"i17"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Aunt Bea is 88, sharp as a tack, and does the crossword in ink. She depends on her nephew Cy for her shopping, her banking and her lifts to the doctor. Over several weeks Cy visits daily and urges her to sell him her house for half its value. He tells her that lawyers \"will only confuse things,\" and one evening he insists she sign before he drives her home. He makes no threats and tells no lies about the house. She signs, with nobody else to advise her. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up?","o":["Lack of mental capacity","Duress by improper threat","Undue influence","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is a dependent person, a trusted relative, steady pressure, a rushed signing, no independent advice and a lopsided price. Under Restatement section 177, a contract is voidable when assent is induced by unfair persuasion from someone who dominates the other party or whom that party is justified in trusting. Lack of capacity is tempting because of her age, but the facts go out of their way to say she is sharp, and duress fails because Cy never threatened anything.","id":"i18"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A couple hires Sky, a drone pilot, to film their wedding in Maple Park from the air for $2,000. The contract calls specifically for aerial drone footage of the ceremony. Two weeks before the wedding, the city passes an ordinance banning all drone flights in public parks, with fines for pilots. Nobody saw the ban coming. Sky tells the couple she cannot fly, and the couple, who have already bought matching drone-themed cufflinks, sue her for breach. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up for Sky?","o":["Frustration of purpose","Mutual mistake about a basic assumption","Unilateral mistake","Impracticability because a new law prohibits the performance"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is an unforeseen event after signing, here a new law, that stops Sky from doing the very thing she promised. Under Restatement sections 261 and 264, a duty is discharged when performance becomes impracticable through an event whose non-occurrence was a basic assumption of the contract, and a new government regulation is a classic example. Frustration of purpose is tempting, but under section 265 that applies when performance is still possible and has merely become pointless, whereas here the performance itself is forbidden.","id":"i19"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"Hugo hires a carpenter to build a garden deck for $12,000, payable on completion. The plans call for a deck twenty feet wide with a chestnut stain. The carpenter finishes a day early, but the deck is nineteen feet ten inches wide and the stain is walnut, a shade darker. It would cost about $300 to restain it. Hugo, who admits the deck is sturdy and handsome, refuses to pay a single dollar because \"it is not what the contract says.\" Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the perfect tender rule allows Hugo to reject the deck","Whether the carpenter substantially performed, so that Hugo must pay the price less damages","Impracticability","Whether the contract price is a penalty"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a finished service with small, cheap-to-fix departures from the plans, and an owner who wants to pay nothing. Under Restatement sections 237 and 241, only a material failure of performance excuses the other party's duty, so a builder who substantially performs can recover the price minus the cost of the defects. Perfect tender is tempting because Hugo demands exact compliance, but that rule belongs to sales of goods under the UCC, and building a deck is a service.","id":"i20"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A bike shop orders forty medium helmets from a distributor for a single delivery on the first of the month. On that day exactly forty medium helmets arrive, but three of them have cracked shells. The shop owner, who has just found a cheaper supplier, sends the entire shipment back the same afternoon. The distributor protests that thirty-seven good helmets out of forty is \"substantial performance\" and demands payment for the lot. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether the buyer may reject the whole shipment because the goods fail in any respect to conform","Whether the seller substantially performed","The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose","Whether this is an installment contract"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is a single delivery of goods with a small defect and a buyer who rejects everything. Under UCC section 2-601, if the goods fail in any respect to conform to the contract, the buyer may reject the whole, accept the whole, or accept any commercial units and reject the rest. Substantial performance is tempting because the seller uses those words, but that is the common law standard for services, and the softer installment rule in section 2-612 does not apply to a one-shot delivery.","id":"i21"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"Nia pays a print shop $60 to print five hundred yard-sale flyers by Friday. The shop delivers them on Monday. Nia then claims $40,000 in damages. She explains, for the first time, that a wealthy collector was flying in on Saturday only, that she had planned to use the flyers to lure him to her yard sale, and that he would have bought her late uncle's painting of a goose. The print shop knew only that the flyers were for a yard sale. The shop admits it was late. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the damages clause is an unenforceable penalty","Whether Nia is entitled to specific performance","Whether the loss was foreseeable to the print shop when the contract was made","Whether the print shop substantially performed"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is a tiny contract, a huge and unusual loss, and special circumstances the breaching party was never told about. Under Restatement section 351, following Hadley and Baxendale, damages are not recoverable for loss the party in breach had no reason to foresee as a probable result of breach when the contract was made. A penalty clause is tempting whenever a number looks outrageous, but this contract has no damages clause at all; the $40,000 is simply what Nia claims she lost.","id":"i22"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A café chain agrees to buy one hundred patio heaters from a manufacturer at $200 each. A week before delivery, after an unusually warm forecast, the chain says it will not take them. The manufacturer tells the chain it intends to resell, and then in good faith sells all one hundred heaters to a garden centre at $150 each, which is a fair price in a falling market. The manufacturer no longer has the heaters. It now wants to recover from the café chain. Which remedy should you analyse first?","o":["The buyer's cover damages","An action for the full contract price","Specific performance","The seller's damages measured by the resale price against the contract price"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is a breaching buyer and a seller who gave notice and then resold the goods in a commercially reasonable way. Under UCC section 2-706, that seller may recover the difference between the contract price and the resale price, plus incidental damages and less expenses saved, which here starts at $50 a heater. An action for the price is tempting, but section 2-709 is mainly for goods the buyer accepted or goods the seller cannot reasonably resell, and these heaters were resold.","id":"i23"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A grandfather signs a contract with a piano teacher and pays $3,000 in advance. The contract says the teacher will give \"weekly lessons for one year to my granddaughter Zoe.\" Zoe is fifteen and signed nothing. After two lessons the teacher moves abroad to join a circus and keeps the money. The grandfather is on a long cruise and has no interest in suing anyone. Zoe, through her mother, sues the teacher. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the grandfather validly assigned his contract rights to Zoe","Whether Zoe is an intended beneficiary who can enforce the contract herself","Whether the teacher improperly delegated the lessons","Whether Zoe lacked capacity because she is a minor"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a plaintiff who never signed the contract but is named in it as the person who receives the performance. Under Restatement section 302, a third party is an intended beneficiary, with her own right to sue, when giving her that right fits the parties' intention and the promisee meant to give her the benefit of the performance. Assignment is tempting, but an assignment is a later transfer of an existing right, and here Zoe's benefit was built into the contract from the start. Her age is a red herring, because capacity matters for people who make contracts, and Zoe did not make one.","id":"i24"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"Lars, a landscaper, finishes replanting a hotel's gardens, and the hotel owes him $4,000. The contract says nothing about transfers. Lars owes his sister Inga money, so he signs a note giving her his right to the hotel's $4,000, and Inga sends the hotel a copy. The hotel manager refuses to pay Inga, saying, \"We never made a contract with you, and we never agreed to this.\" The gardens look splendid and Lars has no work left to do. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether a right to payment can be assigned without the obligor's consent","Whether Inga is an intended third-party beneficiary of the landscaping contract","Whether Lars's duties were too personal to delegate","The statute of frauds"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is a finished job, a simple right to be paid, and the holder of that right handing it to someone else afterwards. Under Restatement section 317, a contract right can generally be assigned without the obligor's consent unless the assignment would materially change the obligor's duty or risk, or is barred by law or by the contract, and paying Inga is no harder than paying Lars. Third-party beneficiary is tempting, but the hotel and Lars did not intend to benefit Inga when they made the contract, and delegation is about handing off duties, which Lars has already performed.","id":"i25"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"By letter, Olga offers to sell her boat to Ravi. On Tuesday Olga mails a revocation. On Wednesday Ravi, knowing nothing of this, mails a properly addressed acceptance. On Thursday Ravi receives the revocation. Is there a contract?","o":["No, because Olga revoked first.","No, because Olga had not yet received the acceptance.","Yes, because the acceptance took effect when mailed, before the revocation was received.","Yes, because an offer made by letter can never be revoked."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 63 an acceptance works on dispatch. A revocation works only on receipt. Ravi posted his acceptance on Wednesday, before the revocation reached him on Thursday, so a contract was formed on Wednesday.","id":"acceptance-m1"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A painter offers to paint Lena's house for $4,000. Lena replies, \"I'll pay $3,500.\" The painter says, \"No thanks,\" and nothing more. Lena then says, \"Fine, I accept your $4,000 offer.\" Is there a contract?","o":["No, because Lena's counteroffer ended her power to accept and the painter did not renew the offer.","Yes, because Lena finally agreed to the painter's price.","Yes, because the original offer was never withdrawn.","No, because painting contracts must be in writing."],"a":0,"w":"Under sections 36 and 59 a counteroffer is a rejection, and it ends the power to accept. In Livingstone and Evans the seller's reply renewed the offer. Here the painter just said no, so there was nothing left to accept.","id":"acceptance-m2"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"An owner says, \"I will pay $1,000 to anyone who paints my whole fence by Friday. I want the job done, not a promise.\" A painter starts on Monday. With half the fence painted, the owner says, \"I revoke.\" What is the result under Restatement section 45?","o":["The revocation works, because the painter had not finished.","The owner must pay $1,000 at once.","The revocation works, because the painter never promised anything.","The owner cannot revoke, and must give the painter the chance to finish and earn the $1,000."],"a":3,"w":"Section 45 says that once the offeree begins the requested performance, an option contract arises. The offeror cannot revoke, but only owes the price if the job is completed. Four-five: once you start, the offer stays alive.","id":"acceptance-m3"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes the general rule on silence as acceptance?","o":["Silence is acceptance whenever the offeror says it will be.","Silence is not acceptance, except in limited cases such as knowingly taking the benefit of offered services or a history of prior dealings.","Silence is acceptance if the offer was made in writing.","Silence is acceptance after thirty days."],"a":1,"w":"Section 69 says silence normally means nothing. An offeror cannot force a contract on you by announcing that no reply means yes. The exceptions are narrow: taking the benefit of services, invited silence you intend as a yes, and prior dealings.","id":"acceptance-m4"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"On Monday, a homeowner offers to sell her land to a neighbour and adds, \"I'll keep this open for you until Saturday.\" The neighbour gives nothing for that assurance. On Wednesday, before any acceptance, she tells the neighbour the offer is withdrawn. Is the revocation effective at common law?","o":["No, because she promised to keep the offer open.","No, because offers to sell land cannot be revoked.","Yes, because without consideration the promise to keep the offer open is not a binding option.","Yes, but only if she has already sold the land to someone else."],"a":2,"w":"This is Dickinson and Dodds. An offer can be revoked any time before acceptance, even if the offeror said it would stay open. Only an option contract, usually bought with consideration under sections 25 and 87, takes away the power to revoke.","id":"acceptance-m5"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"A web designer builds a site for a client, who owes her $5,000. The designer assigns her right to the $5,000 to her landlord. When the landlord demands payment, the client shows that the site was never finished. What is the landlord's position?","o":["The landlord takes free of the client's complaint, because the landlord did nothing wrong.","The assignment is void, because rights to money cannot be assigned.","The landlord takes the right subject to the defences the client had against the designer.","The landlord can sue the client for the full $5,000 and the client must then sue the designer."],"a":2,"w":"An assignee steps into the assignor's shoes. Under section 317 the right passes to the landlord, but it is the same right, with the same weaknesses. Whatever defence the obligor had against the assignor works against the assignee.","id":"assignment-m1"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"A gardener contracts to weed a homeowner's borders every week, then delegates the job to a competent friend. The friend stops turning up. The homeowner never agreed to release the gardener. Whom can the homeowner hold liable?","o":["The gardener, who remains liable on the original contract.","No one, because the duty was validly delegated.","Only the friend, because the gardener dropped out of the contract.","The gardener, but only if the friend cannot pay."],"a":0,"w":"Section 318: delegating a duty does not discharge the delegator. Three-one-eight, you still carry the weight. The gardener is released only if the homeowner agrees to a novation.","id":"assignment-m2"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"What distinguishes a novation from an ordinary delegation of duties?","o":["A novation transfers only rights, never duties.","A novation must be approved by a court.","A novation happens automatically whenever a duty is delegated.","A novation is a new contract, agreed by all the parties, that substitutes a new party and releases the old one."],"a":3,"w":"A delegation is done by one party alone, and leaves the delegator liable. A novation is a fresh agreement, needing the consent of everyone involved, under which the new party takes over and the original party is discharged.","id":"assignment-m3"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"A gallery commissions a celebrated sculptor to carve a statue. The sculptor, busy with other work, hands the commission to a talented former student without asking the gallery. Is the delegation effective?","o":["Yes, because any duty may be delegated to a competent person.","No, because the duty depends on the sculptor's personal skill and reputation.","Yes, provided the student charges the same price.","No, because duties under a contract can never be delegated."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 318, a duty cannot be delegated where the other party has a substantial interest in having that particular person perform. The gallery bargained for this sculptor's hands, not for a sculpture by someone else.","id":"assignment-m4"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"both","q":"A coffee roaster appoints a firm as its exclusive distributor in one state, with a duty to use best efforts to sell the roaster's beans. The firm is then bought by a company owned by the roaster's biggest competitor, which takes over the distributorship. Under the majority's approach in Sally Beauty v. Nexxus Products, may the roaster refuse the substitution?","o":["No, because UCC 2-210 allows every duty to be delegated.","No, unless the roaster first proves that sales have actually fallen.","Yes, because the roaster has a substantial interest in not having its best-efforts duty performed by a competitor's subsidiary.","Yes, because distributorships are outside the UCC altogether."],"a":2,"w":"UCC section 2-210 lets a party perform through a delegate unless the other side has a substantial interest in having the original promisor perform. The Sally Beauty majority held that best efforts under an exclusive distributorship cannot be delegated to a competitor's subsidiary without consent. Judge Posner, dissenting, would have required proof of real harm.","id":"assignment-m5"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"An aunt tells her adult niece, \"If you stay off all social media for one year, I will pay you $2,000.\" The niece does so. The aunt refuses to pay, saying she gained nothing from it. Is the promise enforceable?","o":["No, because the aunt received no benefit.","Yes, because the niece gave up something she had a legal right to do, at the aunt's request.","No, because family promises are never enforceable.","Yes, because all promises of money are enforceable."],"a":1,"w":"This is Hamer and Sidway. Giving up a legal right at the promisor's request is forbearance, and under section 71 forbearance is good consideration. It does not matter whether the promisor got any benefit.","id":"bargain-m1"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"While Priya is abroad, her neighbour repairs Priya's storm-damaged fence without being asked. When Priya returns she says, \"That was kind. I'll pay you $300 for it.\" She never pays. Can the neighbour enforce the promise under the traditional rule?","o":["Yes, because the neighbour did valuable work.","Yes, because Priya's promise was clear and definite.","No, because the promise was not in writing.","No, because the work was done before the promise and was not given in exchange for it."],"a":3,"w":"Past consideration is no consideration. As in Mills and Wyman, the work was finished before any promise was made, so it could not be the price of that promise. A moral duty to pay is not enough.","id":"bargain-m2"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Sam agrees to sell his vintage guitar, worth about $4,000, to Dana for $500. Before delivery Sam regrets the deal and argues the contract fails for lack of consideration. Who is right?","o":["Dana, because courts do not ask whether the consideration was adequate.","Sam, because $500 is far too little.","Sam, because consideration must be roughly equal in value.","Dana, but only if she pays the full $4,000."],"a":0,"w":"Section 79 says there is no requirement of equal value. If the price was truly bargained for, a court will not weigh it. Seven-nine: a peppercorn is fine. A huge gap matters only as a clue to something like fraud or duress.","id":"bargain-m3"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 71, when is a performance or return promise \"bargained for\"?","o":["When it has a market value greater than zero.","When it benefits the promisor financially.","When the promisor seeks it in exchange for the promise and the promisee gives it in exchange for that promise.","When it is written down and signed by both sides."],"a":2,"w":"Section 71 defines consideration as something bargained for: sought by the promisor in exchange for the promise, and given by the promisee in exchange for it. Value alone, benefit alone, or writing alone will not do.","id":"bargain-m4"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"A manager tells a junior colleague, \"Stop by my office on Friday and you can have my old laptop.\" The colleague stops by, but the manager has given the laptop to someone else. Why is the promise most likely unenforceable?","o":["Because laptops cannot be the subject of a contract.","Because stopping by was only a condition of collecting a gift, not something the manager bargained for.","Because the colleague arrived too late in the day.","Because the promise was made at work."],"a":1,"w":"This is a conditional gift. Coming to the office is just how the gift gets collected. Under section 71 consideration must be sought by the promisor as the price of the promise, and the manager was not bargaining for a visit.","id":"bargain-m5"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A city hires a contractor to build a new stadium by March. A café across the street expects a flood of customers. The contractor finishes a year late, and the café sues the contractor for its lost profits. What result?","o":["The café wins, because it was foreseeably harmed.","The café wins, because it is a creditor beneficiary.","The café loses, because only the city's residents can sue.","The café loses, because it is only an incidental beneficiary of the contract."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 302, only an intended beneficiary can enforce a contract. The city and the contractor did not make their deal in order to benefit the café. Its gain would have been a happy accident, so it is incidental.","id":"beneficiaries-m1"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A father buys a car from a dealer on credit, and the dealer promises to deliver it to the father's daughter. The father never pays a cent. The daughter sues the dealer for the car. Can the dealer rely on the father's non-payment?","o":["No, because the daughter did nothing wrong.","Yes, because the promisor can raise against the beneficiary the defences it has under the contract.","No, because defences are personal to the promisee.","Yes, but only if the daughter knew about the non-payment."],"a":1,"w":"Section 309: the beneficiary's right comes through the contract, so it is subject to the promisor's contract defences. If the promisee's failure to perform excuses the promisor, it excuses the promisor against the beneficiary as well.","id":"beneficiaries-m2"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"Priya owes Quinn $500. Priya sells her bike to Rob, and in return Rob promises Priya that he will pay the $500 price directly to Quinn. Rob does not pay. What is Quinn?","o":["A creditor beneficiary, who can sue Rob on his promise.","An incidental beneficiary, who cannot sue anyone.","A donee beneficiary, who can sue only Priya.","An assignee of the bike."],"a":0,"w":"This is Lawrence and Fox. Rob's performance would pay off a debt the promisee, Priya, owes to Quinn. That makes Quinn an intended beneficiary of the creditor kind, and Quinn can sue the promisor directly.","id":"beneficiaries-m3"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes when a third party is an intended beneficiary?","o":["Whenever the third party would be better off if the contract were performed.","Only when the third party is named in the contract and has signed it.","When giving the third party a right to performance carries out what the contracting parties intended.","Only when the third party paid part of the consideration."],"a":2,"w":"Section 302 turns on the intention of the parties. The beneficiary need not sign, pay, or even know about the contract at the time. But a mere benefit, without that intention, is incidental.","id":"beneficiaries-m4"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A grandmother pays a carpenter $4,000, and the carpenter promises her that he will build a treehouse in her grandson's garden as a birthday present. The carpenter never builds it. What is the grandson?","o":["A creditor beneficiary, because the grandmother owed him a treehouse.","A donee beneficiary, who can enforce the carpenter's promise.","An incidental beneficiary, because he paid nothing.","A promisee, because the treehouse was for him."],"a":1,"w":"The grandmother meant the performance as a gift to her grandson, like the aunt in Seaver and Ransom. He is an intended beneficiary of the donee kind under section 302. It does not matter that he gave no consideration.","id":"beneficiaries-m5"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"Dana joins a gym by signing its standard two-page membership form without reading it. The form requires 30 days' written notice to cancel, which is typical for gyms. Dana later says she is not bound because she never read that term. Is she bound?","o":["No, because a term is binding only if it was actually read.","No, because standard forms are never enforceable against consumers.","Yes, because signing a standard form adopts its terms, read or not.","Yes, but only if the gym read the term aloud to her."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 211, a person who signs a standard form adopts its terms whether or not she read them. An ordinary notice clause is nothing the gym had reason to think she would refuse. Not reading is not a defence.","id":"boilerplate-m1"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 211(3), when is a term in a standardized agreement NOT part of the agreement?","o":["When the drafter has reason to believe the other party would not have assented had they known the term was there.","Whenever the other party did not read the form.","Whenever the form was offered on a take-it-or-leave-it basis.","When the term appears on the back of the form."],"a":0,"w":"Section 211, subsection three, removes a term when the drafter has reason to believe the signer would not agree if they knew about it. If they know you'd say no, the term must go. Merely failing to read, or having no chance to bargain, is not enough.","id":"boilerplate-m2"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"In Carnival Cruise Lines v. Shute, why did the majority enforce the forum selection clause printed on the ticket?","o":["Because the Shutes had personally negotiated the clause.","Because consumers can never challenge a forum clause.","Because the injury happened in Florida.","Because the clause was reasonable: it limited where the line could be sued, cut costs, and was not adopted in bad faith."],"a":3,"w":"The Court accepted that the clause was not negotiated, but found it reasonable. One home court saves litigation costs, passengers benefit through lower fares, and Florida was no remote alien forum. The injury actually happened off the coast of Mexico.","id":"boilerplate-m3"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"What is the key difference between the forum selection clause in The Bremen v. Zapata and the one in Carnival Cruise Lines v. Shute?","o":["The Bremen clause was oral; the Carnival clause was written.","The Bremen clause was bargained over by sophisticated businesses; the Carnival clause was fine print on a consumer ticket.","The Bremen clause was struck down; the Carnival clause was enforced.","The Bremen clause chose a United States court; the Carnival clause chose a foreign court."],"a":1,"w":"In The Bremen, two commercial parties of roughly equal strength negotiated the London forum clause, and it was enforced. Carnival applied that approach to a form ticket that nobody negotiated. That stretch is exactly what the dissent objected to.","id":"boilerplate-m4"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"A phone contract states the monthly price on page one. On page 31, in small print, it limits the company's liability for any loss. Under Rakoff's approach to contracts of adhesion, how should these two terms be treated?","o":["Both are fully binding, because the customer signed.","Neither is binding, because the contract was not negotiated.","The price is a visible term and binds; the liability limit is an invisible term that should give way to background law unless justified.","The liability limit binds, but the price can be reset by the court."],"a":2,"w":"Rakoff separates visible terms, the ones customers actually shop on, from invisible fine print. Visible terms are enforced. Invisible terms should yield to the ordinary background law unless the drafter can justify them. Visible, you chose. Invisible, who knows?","id":"boilerplate-m5"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Jo, aged 17, buys a laptop for $900. Three months later it is scratched and worth $500. Still 17, Jo returns it and demands her $900. Under the approach in Halbman v. Lemke, what does Jo recover?","o":["Nothing, because she used the laptop.","$900, because a disaffirming minor is not charged for use or depreciation.","$500, the laptop's present value.","$400, the amount of the depreciation."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 14 a minor's contract is voidable at the minor's option. In Halbman and Lemke the court said a minor who disaffirms hands back what is left and is not charged for use or depreciation. Making her pay would enforce the contract by the back door.","id":"capacity-m1"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes a contract made by a 16-year-old for a non-necessary item?","o":["It is voidable at the minor's option.","It is void from the start and binds no one.","It is voidable by either party.","It is fully binding if the price was fair."],"a":0,"w":"Section 14 makes a minor's contract voidable, not void. The choice belongs to the minor alone, and it does not matter that the deal was fair. Picture the fourteen-year-old signing in pencil.","id":"capacity-m2"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"A dealer agrees to sell a motorbike to Sam. Before delivery, the dealer gets a better offer, learns that Sam is 17, and refuses to deliver, saying the contract is invalid. Sam wants the bike. Who is right?","o":["The dealer, because a minor's contract is void.","The dealer, because either side may cancel a voidable contract.","Neither, because the contract needs a parent's signature.","Sam, because only the minor has the power to avoid the contract."],"a":3,"w":"Voidable is not void. The contract stands unless the protected person cancels it, and only the minor holds that power. The adult is bound. The rule is a shield for the minor, not an escape hatch for the dealer.","id":"capacity-m3"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Ray has a serious mental illness. During an episode he fully understands that he is selling his house for half its value, but his illness leaves him unable to act reasonably about it. The buyer knows of Ray's condition. Can Ray avoid the sale?","o":["No, because he understood the transaction.","No, unless a court had already declared him incompetent.","Yes, because he could not act reasonably and the buyer had reason to know.","Yes, because any contract made by a person with a mental illness is void."],"a":2,"w":"Section 15 has two routes. One: he could not understand the deal. Two: he could not act reasonably about it, and the other side had reason to know. Ray fits the second, which is the modern test from Ortelere.","id":"capacity-m4"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Lee, aged 17 and living on his own, receives emergency medical treatment and signs the clinic's payment form. He later refuses to pay, saying he was a minor. What is the most likely result?","o":["Lee owes nothing, because minors can disaffirm any contract.","Lee owes the reasonable value of the treatment, because it was a necessary.","Lee owes whatever price the form states, however high.","The clinic must sue Lee's parents and no one else."],"a":1,"w":"Necessaries are the N in N, E, S. A minor who receives things such as food, clothing or medical care must pay their reasonable value. Note that it is the reasonable value, not necessarily the contract price.","id":"capacity-m5"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A painter agrees to paint a house for $9,000. The job would cost him $6,000 in labour and paint. The owner cancels before he buys anything or starts work. What are the painter's expectation damages?","o":["$9,000, the full contract price.","$6,000, the cost of doing the job.","$3,000, the profit he would have made.","Nothing, because he had not started."],"a":2,"w":"Section 347 takes the value he lost, nine thousand dollars, and subtracts the six thousand in costs he avoided. That leaves his three thousand dollar profit. Expectation puts him where performance would have, no better.","id":"damages-m1"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"Which interest is protected by making the breaching party hand back a benefit that the injured party conferred on them?","o":["The restitution interest.","The expectation interest.","The reliance interest.","The punitive interest."],"a":0,"w":"Section 344 lists three interests. Restitution looks at what the breacher gained, and makes them give it back. Expectation and reliance both look at what the injured party lost.","id":"damages-m2"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A dentist guarantees a patient \"a perfect smile\" from a painful procedure. The procedure hurts exactly as expected, but the result is worse than before. Under the reasoning of Hawkins v. McGee, why is the ordinary pain of the procedure left out of expectation damages?","o":["Because pain can never be valued in money.","Because the patient signed a consent form.","Because dentists cannot make binding promises.","Because the patient would have suffered that pain even if the promise had been kept."],"a":3,"w":"Hawkins and McGee measures the value promised minus the value received. Pain the patient would have gone through anyway is part of the price of the bargain. It was not caused by the breach.","id":"damages-m3"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A promoter signs a singer for a one-night show at a brand-new venue, then pays $8,000 in non-refundable deposits. The singer cancels. No one can say how many tickets would have sold. What is the promoter's most realistic recovery?","o":["Expectation damages for the full lost profit.","Reliance damages of $8,000.","Punitive damages to deter singers from cancelling.","Nothing, because the profit cannot be proved."],"a":1,"w":"When lost profits are too speculative, the injured party can fall back on the reliance interest in section 344. That is the money spent because of the contract. Here, the eight thousand dollars in deposits.","id":"damages-m4"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A buyer pays $1,000 in advance for a watch that is worth $1,800 on the market. The seller keeps the money and never delivers. Using the points A, B and C, what does the contract expectation measure give the buyer?","o":["$800, the lost bargain only.","$1,000, the return to where she started.","$1,800, the distance from A to C.","$2,800, the price plus the market value."],"a":2,"w":"She is at A, one thousand down. Performance would put her at C, eight hundred up. C minus A is what they pay: one thousand eight hundred dollars. Going back only to B would be a tort measure.","id":"damages-m5"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A painter agrees to paint an office for $8,000. Halfway through, with nothing about the job changed, the painter says, \"Promise me $10,000 or I walk.\" The owner, with a grand opening in two days, says yes. The painter finishes. Under the traditional rule, how much is owed?","o":["$10,000, because the owner agreed to it.","$10,000, because the painter finished the job.","$8,000, because the painter gave nothing new for the extra promise.","Nothing, because the painter's threat cancels the whole contract."],"a":2,"w":"This is Alaska Packers on dry land. Under section 73, doing what you already owe is not consideration, so the promise of the extra two thousand dollars is not enforceable. The owner could also argue economic duress.","id":"duress-m1"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 175, a contract is voidable for duress when a party's assent is induced by:","o":["An improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative.","Any threat at all, however minor.","A price that turns out to be unfair.","Financial need that makes the party desperate for a deal."],"a":0,"w":"Section 175 needs both halves. T, I, N, A: a threat, that is improper, leaving no alternative. A bad price or a tight spot, without an improper threat from the other side, is not duress.","id":"duress-m2"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"After a storm, a homeowner badly needs a generator. The only shop with stock charges $3,000, well above the usual price, and says, \"That's the price.\" The homeowner pays, then claims duress. What is the likely result?","o":["Duress, because the homeowner had no alternative.","Duress, because the price was above market.","Duress, because the shop knew the homeowner was desperate.","No duress, because the shop made no improper threat."],"a":3,"w":"Hard bargaining is not duress. The storm created the pressure, not the shop, and take it or leave it is not an improper threat under section 176. Other doctrines might help the homeowner, but duress will not.","id":"duress-m3"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A supplier tells a manufacturer, \"Pay 20% more or I stop delivering.\" The manufacturer could buy identical parts from another supplier the next day at a slightly higher price, but agrees anyway. Later it sues to recover the increase as paid under duress. What result?","o":["It wins, because a threat to breach is always duress.","It loses, because it had a reasonable alternative.","It wins, because the supplier acted in bad faith.","It loses, because duress applies only to physical threats."],"a":1,"w":"Compare Austin Instrument and Loral, where the buyer rang ten suppliers and none could deliver in time. Here the goods were available elsewhere. With a reasonable alternative, section 175 is not met, even if the threat was improper.","id":"duress-m4"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"Which of these threats is improper under Restatement section 176?","o":["\"Accept my price or I'll sell to someone else.\"","\"Pay the debt you honestly owe me or I'll sue.\"","\"Sign this contract or I'll report you to the police for that old offence.\"","\"I won't renew our contract when it ends unless you pay more.\""],"a":2,"w":"Section 176 lists a threat of criminal prosecution as improper, even if the person is guilty. One seven six: crimes, torts and bad-faith tricks. Refusing to deal, or suing in good faith on a real debt, is ordinary bargaining.","id":"duress-m5"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"An uncle tells his nephew, \"I'm proud of you. I'll give you $10,000 toward a car next month.\" Relying on this, the nephew signs a purchase contract and pays a non-refundable deposit, as the uncle knew he would. The uncle then refuses to pay. What is the nephew's best theory?","o":["Breach of contract, because the uncle's pride was consideration.","Promissory estoppel, because he reasonably and foreseeably relied on the promise to his cost.","No theory at all, because gift promises are never enforceable.","Breach of contract, because the promise named a definite sum."],"a":1,"w":"There is no bargain here, so no consideration. But section 90 makes a promise binding when the promisor should expect reliance, the reliance happens, and injustice would otherwise result. Run PRIDE, and every element is met.","id":"estoppel-m1"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"What does Restatement section 90 say about the remedy for promissory estoppel?","o":["The court must always award full expectation damages.","The court may only order the promise to be performed.","No money may be awarded, only an apology.","The remedy may be limited as justice requires, so a court may award only reliance losses."],"a":3,"w":"Section 90 ends by saying the remedy may be limited as justice requires. Some courts enforce the full promise, as in Feinberg and Pfeiffer. Many award only what was lost in reliance, as in Elvin Associates and Franklin.","id":"estoppel-m2"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"A manager tells a job applicant, \"We might have an opening next year. Stay in touch.\" The applicant turns down a firm job offer elsewhere. No opening ever appears. Will promissory estoppel help the applicant?","o":["No, because there was no clear promise on which it was reasonable to rely.","Yes, because she relied to her detriment.","Yes, because the manager should have foreseen her disappointment.","No, because estoppel never applies to employment."],"a":0,"w":"The first element of section 90 is a promise. A statement that something might happen is not a commitment, and relying on a maybe is not reasonable. Detriment alone is never enough.","id":"estoppel-m3"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"A friend says, \"I'm going to give you my old couch next week.\" You are pleased but do nothing differently. The friend then gives the couch to someone else. Why does a promissory estoppel claim fail?","o":["Because couches are goods.","Because the promise was not in writing.","Because you did not rely on the promise in any way that cost you something.","Because friends cannot make promises to each other."],"a":2,"w":"Section 90 needs action or forbearance induced by the promise, and a real cost. Here there was a promise but no reliance and no detriment. In PRIDE terms, the I and the D are missing.","id":"estoppel-m4"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes how promissory estoppel relates to consideration?","o":["It is another name for consideration.","It applies only when there is already a valid contract.","It lets a court enforce any promise that seems morally deserving.","It can make a promise enforceable without consideration, where the promise induced reasonable reliance."],"a":3,"w":"Promissory estoppel is plan B. When a promise lacks consideration but the promisee reasonably relied on it at real cost, section 90 can make it binding. It is not a general licence to enforce every promise that sounds deserving.","id":"estoppel-m5"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A courier hands a homeowner a tablet and says, \"Just sign to confirm delivery.\" The screen shows only a signature box, and there is no chance to see anything else. In fact the signature is attached to a three-year service contract. What is the status of that contract?","o":["Valid, because a signature always binds the signer.","Void, because this is fraud in the inception: he did not know he was signing a contract at all.","Voidable, because this is fraud in the inducement.","Valid, unless the homeowner can prove he suffered a loss."],"a":1,"w":"When someone is deceived about the very nature of what he is signing, there is no assent at all. That is fraud in the inception, and the contract is void, as in Duick and Toyota. Fraud in the inducement is different: you know it is a contract, but lies talked you into it.","id":"fraud-m1"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A seller honestly believes the roof of her house was replaced last year, and tells the buyer so. She is wrong: the roof is twenty years old. The buyer relied on her statement in agreeing to the price. Can the buyer avoid the contract?","o":["No, because the seller did not intend to deceive.","No, because buyers must always inspect for themselves.","Yes, because a material misrepresentation, justifiably relied on, makes the contract voidable even if it was innocent.","Yes, but only if the buyer first proves the seller was reckless."],"a":2,"w":"Section 164 says fraudulent or material. A statement about the age of the roof would matter to any reasonable buyer, so it is material, and the buyer was justified in relying on it. One-six-four: lied to, even innocently? There's the door.","id":"fraud-m2"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A used-car dealer tells a customer, \"This is the best little truck in the county. You'll love it.\" The truck turns out to be thoroughly ordinary. Can the customer avoid the sale for misrepresentation?","o":["No, because the statement was opinion and sales puffery, not a statement of fact.","Yes, because the statement was false.","Yes, because dealers always have superior knowledge.","No, because misrepresentation never applies to used goods."],"a":0,"w":"A misrepresentation must be a false statement of fact. Loose praise like best in the county is puffery, and a reasonable buyer does not rely on it. It would be different if the dealer had said the truck had never been in an accident.","id":"fraud-m3"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A professional appraiser tells a first-time collector, \"In my expert opinion this vase is museum quality and you will easily resell it for double.\" The appraiser knows the vase is a common reproduction. The collector buys it. The appraiser's best defence is that he only gave an opinion. Will that defence work?","o":["Yes, because opinions can never be misrepresentations.","Yes, because the collector could have hired a second appraiser.","No, because every false opinion is automatically fraud.","No, because an opinion from a party with superior knowledge, who knows the facts do not support it, can be relied on."],"a":3,"w":"This is Vokes and Arthur Murray with a vase. Opinion is normally safe, but not when the speaker has superior knowledge and the listener reasonably trusts it. An expert who states an opinion he knows is baseless is implying facts that are false.","id":"fraud-m4"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A trustee who manages land for a beneficiary offers to buy it from her at last year's valuation. He does not mention that he has just learned a new railway station will be built next door, which will triple its value. He tells no lies. She agrees. Can she avoid the sale?","o":["No, because silence is never a misrepresentation.","No, because of caveat emptor.","Yes, because a fiduciary has a duty to disclose material facts, so his silence is treated as a misrepresentation.","Yes, because all contracts between a trustee and a beneficiary are void."],"a":2,"w":"Ordinarily a party may keep quiet. But a fiduciary, a person in a relation of trust, must speak, and under section 161 his silence counts as an assertion that the fact does not exist. That was the reasoning in Wal-Mart Stores and Coughlin. The sale is voidable.","id":"fraud-m5"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"A restaurant owner orally promises a chef, \"You have a job here for the rest of your life.\" Two years later the chef is fired and sues. The owner raises the one-year provision of the statute of frauds. Does it bar the claim?","o":["Yes, because the chef has already worked more than a year.","Yes, because lifetime contracts always last longer than a year.","No, because the contract could have been fully performed within a year if the chef had died.","No, because employment contracts are never within the statute."],"a":2,"w":"Section 130 covers only contracts that cannot possibly be performed within a year of their making. A lifetime contract could be completed within a year, because a life can end at any time. If it can be done within a year, the statute is not here.","id":"frauds-m1"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"A contract within the statute of frauds is recorded in a memo signed only by the buyer. The buyer sues the seller, who signed nothing. Can the buyer enforce the contract against the seller on the strength of that memo?","o":["No, because the writing must be signed by the party to be charged, here the seller.","Yes, because one signature is enough for both parties.","Yes, because the buyer wrote the memo.","No, because both parties must always sign the same page."],"a":0,"w":"Under section 131 the writing must be signed by the party to be charged, meaning the person against whom enforcement is sought. The seller signed nothing, so the memo does not satisfy the statute against the seller. It would work against the buyer.","id":"frauds-m2"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"A nephew borrows $8,000 from a bank. His uncle telephones the bank and says, \"If he doesn't pay, I will.\" The nephew defaults and the bank sues the uncle. What is the uncle's best defence?","o":["The promise was made by telephone, so it was never an offer.","The debt was under $10,000.","Only contracts for land need a writing.","A promise to answer for another person's debt is within the statute of frauds and needs a signed writing."],"a":3,"w":"This is suretyship, the S in my legs. Under section 110 a promise to pay the debt of another must be evidenced by a writing signed by the promisor. The uncle's oral promise is not enforceable against him.","id":"frauds-m3"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"On January 1, a designer orally agrees to work for a studio for eleven months, starting on March 1. Is the agreement within the one-year provision?","o":["No, because the work itself lasts less than a year.","Yes, because performance cannot be completed within a year of the making of the contract.","No, because the year is measured from the first day of work.","Yes, because all employment contracts need a writing."],"a":1,"w":"The year is measured from the making of the contract, not from the start of performance. Eleven months of work beginning two months after the deal ends thirteen months after it was made. Under section 130 it needs a writing.","id":"frauds-m4"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"An employee's two-year oral contract is reflected in an unsigned office memo listing the terms, and in two payroll forms signed by company officers that state his salary. Under Crabtree v. Elizabeth Arden Sales, can these satisfy the statute of frauds?","o":["Yes, because separate writings may be read together when they clearly relate to the same transaction and at least one is signed by the party to be charged.","No, because the statute requires one single signed document.","No, because payroll forms are not contracts.","Yes, because an unsigned memo is always enough."],"a":0,"w":"Crabtree held that the memorandum may be pieced together from several writings. The signed payroll forms and the unsigned memo plainly referred to the same employment deal, so together they supplied the essential terms and the signature.","id":"frauds-m5"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"An artist agrees to paint a mural on the side of a farmer's old barn. The night before she is due to start, lightning strikes and the barn burns to the ground. Neither party is at fault, and the contract says nothing about fire. Is the artist liable for not painting the mural?","o":["Yes, because promises must be kept.","Yes, unless she offers to paint a different building.","No, because the thing necessary for performance was destroyed without her fault.","No, because contracts for art are never enforceable."],"a":2,"w":"This is Taylor and Caldwell with a barn. The contract assumed the barn would exist. When the thing essential to performance is destroyed and nobody is to blame, section 261 discharges the duty. Two-six-one: it cannot be done.","id":"impossibility-m1"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A contractor agrees to build a garage for a fixed price of $60,000. Halfway through, timber prices rise and the job will now cost her $12,000 more than she planned. Is she excused from finishing?","o":["No. An ordinary rise in cost is a risk a fixed-price contract puts on the contractor.","Yes, because performance has become impracticable.","Yes, because her purpose of making a profit has been frustrated.","No, unless the owner agrees that timber is too expensive."],"a":0,"w":"A mere increase in cost is not impracticability. Market swings are exactly the risk that a fixed price hands to the party who quoted it. And losing your profit is not frustration of purpose, or nobody would ever finish a bad deal.","id":"impossibility-m2"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A fan pays a deposit to rent a balcony for one afternoon, at ten times the usual rate, to watch a championship victory parade. The owner advertised the balcony for exactly that. The city then cancels the parade. The balcony is still available. Which doctrine best helps the fan avoid paying the balance?","o":["Impracticability, because the fan cannot use the balcony.","Mutual mistake, because both expected a parade.","Unconscionability, because the price was ten times the usual rate.","Frustration of purpose, because the shared reason for the deal has been destroyed."],"a":3,"w":"This is Krell and Henry. The fan can still stand on the balcony, so nothing is impossible. But the parade was the foundation of the deal, as both sides knew, and it is gone. That is section 265, frustration of purpose.","id":"impossibility-m3"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best captures the difference between Restatement §261 and §265?","o":["§261 applies to goods and §265 applies to services.","Under §261 performance itself has become impracticable; under §265 performance is still possible but its principal purpose is gone.","§261 requires fault and §265 does not.","§261 covers events before signing and §265 covers events after signing."],"a":1,"w":"Section 261 says, I cannot do it. Section 265 says, I can, but why bother? Both deal with events after the contract is made, both require no fault, and both require that the event upset a basic assumption of the deal.","id":"impossibility-m4"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A restaurant's lease excuses performance for \"fire, flood, war, labour strikes, or other similar causes.\" The restaurant stops paying rent after its largest catering customer goes bankrupt, and points to the clause. Is it excused?","o":["Yes, because the bankruptcy was beyond its control.","Yes, because force majeure clauses are read broadly in favour of tenants.","No, because losing a customer is not listed and is not similar to the listed events.","No, because force majeure clauses are never enforceable."],"a":2,"w":"Courts read force majeure clauses narrowly, as in Kel Kim and Central Markets. Only the listed events excuse, and catch-all words reach only things of the same kind. A customer's bankruptcy is an ordinary business risk, nothing like a fire or a flood.","id":"impossibility-m5"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A printed lease says, in its general rules, \"No animals may be kept on the premises.\" A clause typed in for this tenant says, \"Tenant may keep one cat.\" The landlord demands the cat go. Which term controls?","o":["The general rule, because it appears first.","Neither; the conflict makes the lease void.","The cat clause, because specific and separately added terms outweigh general standard language.","The general rule, because printed terms outweigh typed ones."],"a":2,"w":"Section 203 sets the pecking order. Specific terms are given greater weight than general language, and terms added for this deal outweigh standard printed ones. The cat stays.","id":"interpretation-m1"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"An insurer drafts a policy excluding \"water damage\". After weighing the whole policy and all the evidence, the phrase could reasonably mean either flooding only or any leak. Neither meaning is favoured by the other rules of interpretation. How should a court read it?","o":["Against the insurer, because it drafted the ambiguous term.","In favour of the insurer, because it knows insurance best.","By striking the whole policy.","By splitting the loss equally."],"a":0,"w":"This is section 206, contra proferentem. When a term remains ambiguous, the meaning that works against the drafter is generally preferred. You wrote the mess, you get less.","id":"interpretation-m2"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"In Frigaliment Importing v. B.N.S. International Sales, why did the buyer lose?","o":["Because the word \"chicken\" has only one possible meaning.","Because the contract was never signed.","Because trade usage is never admissible.","Because the buyer failed to prove that the contract used \"chicken\" in the narrower sense of young birds."],"a":3,"w":"The word was ambiguous, and the evidence pointed both ways. The buyer, who claimed the narrower meaning, bore the burden of proving it and fell short. The contract price, too low for young birds, counted against him.","id":"interpretation-m3"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A written supply contract says, \"Delivery by 9 a.m. each Monday.\" In this industry, the custom is to deliver by noon. The supplier delivers at 11:30 a.m. and points to the custom. Who is right?","o":["The supplier, because trade usage always controls.","The buyer, because an express term outranks trade usage.","The supplier, because noon is close enough.","Neither, because the contract is too uncertain."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 203, express terms sit at the top of the ladder, above course of performance, course of dealing and trade usage. Eat pizza during trials. Custom fills gaps, but it cannot override what the parties actually wrote.","id":"interpretation-m4"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"Clause 4 of a contract sets a 15% royalty on all sales. Clause 5 begins, \"Notwithstanding the foregoing, the royalty on licensed uses is 50%.\" A use fits both clauses. Which rate applies?","o":["50%, because \"notwithstanding the foregoing\" makes clause 5 override what came before it.","15%, because the earlier clause controls.","The average of the two rates.","Neither, because the clauses cancel each other out."],"a":0,"w":"That was the lesson of F.B.T. Productions and Aftermath Records. Notwithstanding the foregoing signals that the clause wins over anything before it. Reading the agreement as a whole under section 202, the fifty percent rate applies.","id":"interpretation-m5"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A print shop hires a technician to repair its only press by Friday. Nothing is said about deadlines. The technician finishes the following Wednesday. Only then does the shop reveal it lost a $30,000 rush order that had to print over the weekend. Can the shop recover the $30,000?","o":["Yes, because the breach in fact caused the loss.","No, because the technician had no reason to foresee that loss when the contract was made.","Yes, because consequential damages are always recoverable.","No, because lost profits can never be recovered in contract."],"a":1,"w":"This is Hadley and Baxendale, now section 351. Special losses are recoverable only if the breaching party had reason to foresee them when the contract was made. Telling the technician afterwards comes too late.","id":"limits-m1"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner hires a builder to construct a garage, then clearly cancels when the work is one-third done. The builder finishes the garage anyway and sues for the full price. What can the builder recover?","o":["The full price, because the builder performed in full.","Nothing, because the builder ignored the cancellation.","The full price, less a small discount for annoying the owner.","Its costs up to the cancellation plus the profit it would have made, but nothing for work done afterwards."],"a":3,"w":"Section 350 and Rockingham County and Luten Bridge. After notice of repudiation, you cannot keep working and run up the bill. The builder gets what it had spent by then, plus its lost profit.","id":"limits-m2"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"When will a court enforce a clause that fixes in advance the amount payable on breach?","o":["When the amount is a reasonable forecast of the loss, given how hard that loss would be to prove.","Whenever both parties signed the contract.","Only when the clause uses the words \"liquidated damages\".","Only when the amount is larger than the actual loss, so that breach is deterred."],"a":0,"w":"Section 356 allows damages to be fixed in advance at an amount that is reasonable in light of the expected or actual loss and the difficulty of proof. A figure set unreasonably high is a penalty, and penalties are unenforceable.","id":"limits-m3"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A first-time restaurateur's landlord wrongfully refuses to hand over the premises. The restaurateur claims $2 million in lost profits, based only on her own optimism. What is the best statement of the law?","o":["A new business can never recover lost profits.","Lost profits are presumed once breach is shown.","A new business may recover lost profits, but only with evidence that establishes them with reasonable certainty.","Lost profits are recoverable only if the contract states the amount."],"a":2,"w":"Section 352 requires reasonable certainty. As MindGames and Western Publishing explains, being a new business is not an automatic bar. But with no track record or other solid basis, the claim is speculation.","id":"limits-m4"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A caterer agrees to cater a company gala for $10,000, then cancels. The company hires a comparable caterer for $13,000. Which item is general damages, as opposed to consequential damages?","o":["Profit lost when a client, unimpressed by the gala, took its business elsewhere.","The $3,000 extra cost of the substitute caterer.","The chief executive's embarrassment.","A bonus the events manager failed to earn."],"a":1,"w":"General damages are the loss in the very thing promised, here the extra cost of getting the same catering elsewhere. Consequential damages are knock-on losses, and under section 351 they must have been foreseeable when the contract was made.","id":"limits-m5"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A seller and a buyer sign a contract for a plot of land. Both believe a house can legally be built on it; that is the whole reason for the sale. Unknown to either, the land had been declared protected wetland a year earlier, and nothing can be built there. The contract says nothing about the risk. What is the buyer's best argument?","o":["Unilateral mistake, because only the buyer is hurt.","Mutual mistake: both were wrong about a basic assumption that materially affects the exchange.","The contract fails for lack of consideration.","There is no argument. A signed contract is final."],"a":1,"w":"This is section 152. Both parties shared a false belief about an existing fact, it was a basic assumption of the deal, and it changes the exchange dramatically. Mutual plus basic plus material equals voidable, so long as the buyer did not bear the risk.","id":"mistake-m1"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"Which of these is NOT a \"mistake\" as Restatement §151 uses the word?","o":["A buyer's belief that the shares he is buying will rise in price next year.","A buyer's belief that the painting in front of him was painted by a famous artist.","A seller's belief that the cow she is selling cannot have calves.","A builder's belief that his written bid includes the cost of the roof."],"a":0,"w":"Section 151 defines a mistake as a belief not in accord with the facts, meaning facts that exist when the contract is made. A belief about what the market will do next year is a prediction. Bad predictions are not mistakes.","id":"mistake-m2"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A subcontractor's bid leaves out an entire floor of wiring because of a spreadsheet error. The bid is 45% lower than every other bid. The general contractor notices, says nothing, and quickly accepts. Can the subcontractor avoid the contract?","o":["No, because only one party was mistaken.","No, because a party always bears the risk of its own arithmetic.","Yes, because any mistake by either party makes a contract void.","Yes, because the other party knew or had reason to know of the mistake."],"a":3,"w":"Section 153 covers unilateral mistake. One side's mistake about a basic assumption with a material effect is enough if the other side knew or should have known of it, or if enforcement would be unconscionable. You may not snap up an offer you know is a slip.","id":"mistake-m3"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"At an estate sale, a buyer pays $50 for a locked trunk sold \"as is, contents unknown.\" He hopes it holds antiques. It holds old newspapers. Can he avoid the contract for mistake?","o":["Yes, because he was mistaken about a basic assumption.","Yes, because the seller was also unaware of the contents.","No, because he knew he did not know what was inside and bought anyway.","No, because mistake never applies to sales under $500."],"a":2,"w":"Section 154 says a party bears the risk when he knows his knowledge is limited and treats it as good enough. That is conscious ignorance, the A in car. He was not mistaken. He was gambling, and he lost.","id":"mistake-m4"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"Two business partners split up. Under their agreement, one takes shares that both value at $1 million, using accurate current figures. A year later the company's market collapses and the shares are worth almost nothing. Can that partner undo the agreement for mutual mistake?","o":["Yes, because both parties were wrong about what the shares were worth.","No, because a later change in value is not a mistake about the facts at the time of the contract.","Yes, because the loss is material.","No, because mistake applies only to sales of goods."],"a":1,"w":"A mistake must concern facts as they stood when the contract was made. Here the shares were worth what the parties thought, and then the world changed. As in Simkin and Blank, a later drop in value is a risk, not a mistake.","id":"mistake-m5"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Two companies sign a letter of intent for the sale of a factory. It says the deal is \"subject to board approval and a definitive purchase agreement.\" No final agreement is ever signed. Is the letter a binding contract of sale?","o":["Yes, because both companies signed it.","No, because the \"subject to\" language shows they were still negotiating.","Yes, because letters of intent are always binding.","No, because factories cannot be sold by letter."],"a":1,"w":"Words like subject to a definitive agreement show the parties did not yet intend to be bound. That was the result in Empro and Ball-Co. A letter of intent can be binding, but not when it is full of escape hatches.","id":"offer-m1"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Mia tells Noah, \"I'll sell you my bike for $200.\" Owen, standing nearby, overhears and says, \"I accept!\" before Noah answers. Has Owen formed a contract with Mia?","o":["Yes, because he accepted first.","Yes, because the price was definite.","No, because the offer was too vague.","No, because only the person the offer was made to can accept it."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 29 the offeror is master of the offer and decides who may accept. Mia invited Noah, not Owen. A bystander who overhears an offer has no power to accept it.","id":"offer-m2"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A dealership's online ad says, \"Used cars from $5,000! Great deals all weekend!\" A customer walks in and demands a car for $5,000. Is the dealership bound?","o":["No, because the ad is an invitation to deal and names no particular car or terms.","Yes, because every advertisement is an offer.","Yes, because the customer came in person.","No, because car sales must always be negotiated."],"a":0,"w":"An ad is an offer only if it is clear, definite, and leaves nothing to negotiate. This one does not say which car, how many, or who gets it. It is an invitation to come in and make an offer.","id":"offer-m3"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A poster reads, \"$100 reward for the safe return of Biscuit, a lost beagle.\" A neighbour who has seen the poster finds Biscuit and brings him home. What best describes the poster?","o":["An invitation to deal with no legal effect.","A gift promise that cannot be enforced.","An offer that could be accepted by performing the requested act.","A bilateral contract formed when the poster went up."],"a":2,"w":"A reward is an offer for a unilateral contract, like the smoke ball advertisement. It asks for an act, not a promise. The neighbour accepted by returning the dog, so the owner owes the reward.","id":"offer-m4"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A landowner says to a friend, \"I'll sell you some of my land at a fair price sometime next year.\" The friend says, \"I accept.\" Why is there most likely no contract?","o":["Because the friend did not pay a deposit.","Because the terms are too uncertain for a court to enforce.","Because offers to sell land are illegal.","Because the landowner was secretly joking."],"a":1,"w":"Section 33 requires terms certain enough for a court to find a breach and give a remedy. Here we do not know which land, what price, or when. Thirty-three: the court cannot see what the deal should be.","id":"offer-m5"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"A shopping site has a small grey \"Terms\" link in the footer of every page. The terms contain an arbitration clause. A customer buys a lamp without ever scrolling to the footer or being told that terms apply. Is the customer bound to arbitrate?","o":["Yes, because using a website always means accepting its terms.","Probably not, because the customer had no reasonable notice that any terms existed.","Yes, because the customer had a duty to read every page.","No, because arbitration clauses are never enforceable online."],"a":1,"w":"This is browsewrap. As in Specht and Netscape, terms bind only if a reasonable user would have noticed them. A faint footer link, with nothing drawing attention to it, gives no reasonable notice, so there is no assent to the arbitration clause.","id":"online-m1"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"To create an account, a user must tick a box beside the words \"I have read and agree to the Terms of Service\", with the terms linked. The user ticks the box without opening the link. Is the user bound by the terms?","o":["No, because the user never opened the terms.","No, because online contracts need a handwritten signature.","Only if the user later reads the terms.","Yes, because ticking the box is an outward sign of assent, and the user was told terms applied."],"a":3,"w":"This is clickwrap. The user had clear notice that terms applied and took an action showing assent. The duty to read means that choosing not to open the link is no excuse. Click, it sticks.","id":"online-m2"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"What does \"inquiry notice\" mean in online contracting?","o":["A reasonable person in the user's position would have realised that terms existed and could have looked them up.","The user actually read and understood every term.","The company emailed the user a copy of the terms after the purchase.","The user asked customer service a question about the terms."],"a":0,"w":"Actual notice means you really knew of the terms. Inquiry notice means the circumstances would alert a reasonable person that terms exist, as with the link under the sign-up button in Fteja and Facebook. Either kind of notice can support assent.","id":"online-m3"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"In Specht v. Netscape, why were the users not bound by the arbitration clause in the software licence?","o":["Because arbitration clauses in free software are illegal.","Because the users were under eighteen.","Because the licence terms were visible only by scrolling below the download button, so a reasonable user would not have known of them.","Because the users clicked \"I do not agree\"."],"a":2,"w":"The licence terms sat below the download button, on a part of the page users had no reason to visit. Clicking download did not clearly show assent to terms nobody was told about. No reasonable notice means no assent.","id":"online-m4"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT a typical feature of arbitration?","o":["Limited rights of appeal.","A jury decides the dispute.","Less discovery than in court.","The decision is usually not explained in a public opinion."],"a":1,"w":"Remember jade: no jury, almost no appeal, limited discovery, and no public explanation. An arbitrator decides, not a jury. That is why it matters so much whether the user ever agreed to the arbitration clause.","id":"online-m5"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Lena buys a used car under a detailed signed contract that says, \"This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties' agreement.\" It does not mention a roof rack. Lena wants to testify that, the day before signing, the seller orally promised to include one. Is the testimony admissible to add the term?","o":["Yes, because oral promises are always enforceable.","No, because a completely integrated writing discharges earlier agreements within its scope.","Yes, because the roof rack does not contradict the writing.","No, because car sales can never include extras."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 213, a completely integrated agreement discharges prior agreements within its scope, even consistent ones. The merger clause is strong evidence of complete integration. A slice can take extras; the whole pie cannot.","id":"parol-m1"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A signed contract for the sale of a boat contains a merger clause. The buyer offers evidence that, before signing, the seller knowingly lied about the engine having been rebuilt. Does the parol evidence rule bar this evidence?","o":["Yes, because the merger clause makes the writing complete.","Yes, because the lie was told before signing.","No, but only if the lie was in writing.","No, because evidence of fraud is admissible even when the writing is integrated."],"a":3,"w":"Section 214 allows evidence of prior negotiations to show fraud, duress, mistake or other invalidating causes. That is the F in firm. The rule protects valid final writings; it does not shelter a contract obtained by fraud.","id":"parol-m2"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Two weeks after signing a fully integrated catering contract, the parties agree by phone to move the event from Friday to Saturday. The caterer later argues the parol evidence rule bars proof of the phone call. Is the caterer right?","o":["No, because the rule covers only agreements made before or at the time of the writing.","Yes, because the writing was fully integrated.","Yes, because phone calls are parol evidence.","No, because catering contracts are exempt from the rule."],"a":0,"w":"The parol evidence rule deals with prior and contemporaneous agreements. A later agreement is a modification, and the rule does not touch it. Whether the modification is itself enforceable is a separate question.","id":"parol-m3"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Under Pacific Gas & Electric v. G.W. Thomas Drayage, when may a court admit extrinsic evidence to interpret a written contract?","o":["Only when the judge finds the words ambiguous on their face.","Never, if the contract is signed.","When the evidence is relevant to prove a meaning to which the language is reasonably susceptible.","Only when both parties consent."],"a":2,"w":"The California court rejected a pure plain-meaning approach, because words take their meaning from context. Evidence comes in if it shows a meaning the language could reasonably bear. New York, in W.W.W. Associates, keeps to the four corners when the writing is clear.","id":"parol-m4"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"In Mitchill v. Lath, why was the sellers' oral promise to remove the icehouse not enforceable?","o":["Because the promise contradicted the price term.","Because it was the kind of term the parties would ordinarily be expected to include in the written contract of sale.","Because promises about buildings are never collateral.","Because Mrs Mitchill had not paid for the farm."],"a":1,"w":"A collateral oral agreement survives only if it is collateral in form, does not contradict the writing, and is not something the parties would ordinarily put in the writing. The icehouse promise failed the third test, because it was so closely tied to the sale of the farm.","id":"parol-m5"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A bakery hires a freelance designer to create a new logo for $3,000. The designer delivers the finished logo as a digital file, and a dispute arises over the fee. Which body of law governs?","o":["UCC Article 2, because the bakery is a merchant.","UCC Article 2, because a file was delivered.","The common law of contracts, because this is a contract for services.","Neither, because creative work cannot be the subject of a contract."],"a":2,"w":"Article 2 covers sales of goods, which UCC section 2-105 defines as movable things. Designing a logo is a service, so the common law applies. Being a business does not pull a service contract into the UCC.","id":"rulebook-m1"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"What is the legal status of the Restatement (Second) of Contracts?","o":["It is persuasive authority that courts often follow, but it is not binding law.","It is a federal statute that binds every court.","It is a state statute adopted everywhere except Louisiana.","It binds only merchants."],"a":0,"w":"The Restatement is a scholarly summary of the common law. Courts respect it and often adopt its rules, but it is not a statute. The Restatement suggests. The UCC commands.","id":"rulebook-m2"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A contractor agrees to refinish a homeowner's floors for $7,000: $6,200 for labour and $800 for varnish and other materials. In a state that uses the predominant purpose test, which law governs the contract?","o":["The UCC governs the varnish and the common law governs the labour.","The UCC, because goods were supplied.","The UCC, because the contractor is a professional.","The common law, because the deal is mainly for services."],"a":3,"w":"The predominant purpose test asks what the deal is mainly about, and then applies one rulebook to the whole contract. Here the labour dwarfs the materials, so it is a service contract. Common law governs all of it.","id":"rulebook-m3"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A salon charges a customer $60 for a haircut and $15 for a bottle of shampoo in a single transaction. The shampoo turns out to be defective. In a state that uses the gravamen of the complaint test, which law applies to the customer's claim?","o":["The common law, because most of the price was for the haircut.","The UCC, because the complaint is about the goods.","The common law, because salons provide services.","Neither, because the amount is too small."],"a":1,"w":"The gravamen test looks at what the complaint is actually about. The problem is the shampoo, which is goods, so the UCC applies. Under the predominant purpose test, the answer could well be different.","id":"rulebook-m4"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A retired teacher sells her own used piano to a neighbour. It is the only piano she has ever sold. Which statement is correct?","o":["The UCC does not apply, because she is not a merchant.","The UCC applies, and she is a merchant because she sold goods.","The UCC applies, but the rules reserved for merchants do not apply to her.","The common law applies, because the piano is used."],"a":2,"w":"Article 2 covers every sale of goods, whoever the parties are. But under UCC section 2-104 a merchant is someone who deals in goods of that kind. A one-off seller is not a merchant, so merchant-only rules stay out.","id":"rulebook-m5"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A seller signs a contract to sell her lakeside cottage, then refuses to close because a higher offer has arrived. The buyer asks the court to order the sale to go through. What is the likely result?","o":["Denied, because the buyer can be paid the difference in price.","Denied, because courts never order anyone to perform a contract.","Granted, because land is treated as unique and money is an inadequate substitute.","Granted, but only if the buyer proves the seller acted in bad faith."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 359, specific performance is available where damages are inadequate. Land is the classic example, because every parcel is treated as unique. No sum of money buys that particular cottage.","id":"specific-m1"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A celebrated chef signs a two-year contract to cook at a restaurant, then walks out after a month. The restaurant asks for an order compelling the chef to return to the kitchen. Why will the court refuse?","o":["Because employment contracts are never binding.","Because courts will not specifically enforce a promise of personal service.","Because the restaurant suffered no loss.","Because only land contracts can ever be specifically enforced."],"a":1,"w":"Section 367 says a promise to render personal service will not be specifically enforced. A court cannot supervise the cooking, and compelled labour raises involuntary servitude concerns under the Thirteenth Amendment. The restaurant is left to damages.","id":"specific-m2"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"According to the reasoning in Van Wagner Advertising v. S&M Enterprises, when is the subject of a contract \"unique\" enough to support specific performance?","o":["Whenever it is physically different from every other item.","Whenever the injured party says it has sentimental value.","Whenever it is an interest in real property, including a lease.","When its value is so uncertain that damages risk under-compensating the injured party."],"a":3,"w":"Van Wagner says almost everything is physically unique. What matters is how uncertain the valuation is. Billboard space had a market price, so damages were adequate, and specific performance was refused.","id":"specific-m3"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"An investor contracts to buy 100 shares of a company whose stock trades every day on a public exchange. The seller refuses to deliver. Will a court order specific performance?","o":["No, because the investor can buy identical shares on the market and recover any price difference in damages.","Yes, because every share certificate has its own number.","Yes, because investment contracts are always specifically enforced.","No, because contracts for shares are not enforceable at all."],"a":0,"w":"Section 360 asks whether a substitute is easy to get and the loss easy to value. Here both are true. Identical shares are on sale every day, so damages are adequate under section 359.","id":"specific-m4"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A court finds that a tenant's lease was wrongfully cancelled. Restoring the lease would block the owner's $50 million redevelopment, while the tenant's loss can be valued at about $30,000. What is the court likely to do?","o":["Order specific performance, because breach always leads to it.","Refuse any remedy, because the owner's project matters more.","Award damages, because equity will not impose hardship far out of proportion to the benefit.","Order specific performance, but only for one year."],"a":2,"w":"Specific performance is an equitable remedy. As in Van Wagner, a court will refuse it where it would impose a burden on the breaching party out of all proportion to the benefit. The tenant still gets damages.","id":"specific-m5"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A contract for a new house calls for \"Volta brand\" wiring. By an honest oversight the electrician installs another brand of identical quality. Replacing it would mean opening every wall at a cost of $40,000. The house's value is unaffected. The owner refuses to pay the final $30,000. What result?","o":["The owner owes nothing, because the contract was not performed exactly.","The builder must replace the wiring before being paid anything.","The owner must pay, less the difference in value, which is little or nothing.","The owner must pay, but may deduct the $40,000 cost of replacement."],"a":2,"w":"This is Jacob and Youngs and Kent with wires for pipes. The deviation was innocent and trivial, so the builder substantially performed. Because the cost of replacement is grossly out of proportion, damages are the difference in value.","id":"substantial-m1"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"When one party to a construction contract has substantially performed, the other party:","o":["Must still perform, but may recover damages for the defects.","May treat the contract as ended and pay nothing.","Must pay the full price with no deduction.","May demand that the work be redone at any cost."],"a":0,"w":"Close enough gets paid, less the damage made. Substantial performance keeps the other side's duty to pay alive, but it does not erase the breach. Only a material breach lets the other party refuse to perform.","id":"substantial-m2"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A company leases land to extract gravel and promises to leave it graded level. To save money, it deliberately leaves the land rutted. Grading would cost $60,000 and the graded land would be worth $12,000. Under Groves v. John Wunder Co., what does the landowner recover?","o":["Nothing, because the land has little value.","$12,000, the value of the graded land.","Only nominal damages.","$60,000, the cost of doing the promised work."],"a":3,"w":"Groves awarded the cost of completion. The breach was wilful, and the court would not let a deliberate breacher shelter behind the low value of the land. Compare Peevyhouse, which went the other way.","id":"substantial-m3"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"In Peevyhouse v. Garland Coal, why did the court limit the farmers to $300?","o":["Because the farmers had not performed their own side of the lease.","Because the cost of restoration was grossly out of proportion to the value it would add, and the promise was treated as incidental.","Because the coal company's breach was innocent.","Because the lease never mentioned restoring the land."],"a":1,"w":"The court called restoration an incidental term and said twenty-nine thousand dollars of work for three hundred dollars of value was economic waste. So it awarded diminution in value. Critics reply that the farmers bargained for that promise and the breach was deliberate.","id":"substantial-m4"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A builder walks off the job leaving a house with no roof and no plumbing, then demands the full contract price less a small allowance. Which statement is most accurate?","o":["This is a material breach, so the owner may withhold payment and treat the contract as ended.","This is substantial performance, because most of the walls are up.","The owner must pay in full and sue separately.","The builder wins, because construction contracts are never forfeited."],"a":0,"w":"Substantial performance needs the purpose of the contract to be served, with only a petty defect. A house with no roof fails that test. This is a material breach, and the owner's duty to pay does not come due.","id":"substantial-m5"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A shop contracts to buy 500 phone cases at $4 each ($2,000). The seller repudiates. On the day the shop learns of this, the market price is $5 each. The shop promptly and reasonably buys 500 substitute cases from another supplier at $5.50 each ($2,750). Ignoring incidentals, what may the shop recover?","o":["$500, the market price minus the contract price.","$750, the cover price minus the contract price.","$2,750, the full cost of the substitute cases.","Nothing, because it ended up with the phone cases it needed."],"a":1,"w":"The shop covered, so section 2-712 applies: cover price minus contract price. Twenty-seven fifty minus two thousand is seven hundred and fifty dollars. The cover only has to be reasonable and in good faith, not the cheapest available.","id":"ucc-buyer-m1"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer contracts for a load of steel at $10,000. The seller repudiates, and the buyer does not buy substitute steel. The market price was $10,000 when the contract was made, $12,000 when the buyer learned of the breach, and $15,000 at trial. What are the buyer's market damages?","o":["$2,000.","$5,000.","$0.","$15,000."],"a":0,"w":"Under section 2-713, a buyer who does not cover gets the market price at the time the buyer learned of the breach, minus the contract price. Twelve thousand minus ten thousand is two thousand. The price at trial is irrelevant.","id":"ucc-buyer-m2"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A seller fails to deliver a standard motor part to a factory, knowing the factory needs it to keep its line running. An identical part was available from a local supplier the next morning, but the factory did nothing for three weeks and now claims three weeks of lost profits. Can it recover them?","o":["Yes, because the seller knew of the factory's needs.","Yes, because consequential damages are automatic under the UCC.","No, because buyers can never recover lost profits.","No, because the loss could reasonably have been prevented by cover."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-715 allows consequential damages only for losses the seller had reason to know about and that could not reasonably be prevented by cover or otherwise. The factory could have covered the next morning. It sat still, so the lost profits are on the factory.","id":"ucc-buyer-m3"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"both","q":"How do the UCC's buyer's remedies relate to common-law expectation damages?","o":["They are unrelated; the UCC aims to punish the breaching seller.","The UCC gives only restitution, while the common law gives expectation.","They share the same goal of putting the buyer where performance would have, and the UCC supplies specific formulas for goods.","The common law gives more, because the UCC bars all consequential damages."],"a":2,"w":"Both systems protect the expectation interest. The UCC simply spells out how to measure it for goods: cover under section 2-712, market under section 2-713, and the extras under section 2-715. Same destination, clearer road signs.","id":"ucc-buyer-m4"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A farmer pays $38,000 for a tractor warranted to pull a certain load. It cannot. The farmer keeps the tractor and promptly notifies the seller. As warranted, the tractor would have been worth $40,000; as delivered, it is worth $31,000. What is the basic measure of damages?","o":["$9,000, the value as warranted minus the value as delivered.","$7,000, the price paid minus the value as delivered.","$38,000, a full refund.","$2,000, the value as warranted minus the price paid."],"a":0,"w":"For goods the buyer has accepted and kept, section 2-714 measures damages as the value the goods would have had as warranted, minus their value as accepted. Forty thousand minus thirty-one thousand is nine thousand. The price paid is not the yardstick.","id":"ucc-buyer-m5"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A wholesaler contracts to deliver 500 standard cardboard boxes to a shop. Without asking the shop, the wholesaler delegates the job to another supplier, who delivers boxes that fall apart. Whom may the shop hold liable for the breach?","o":["Nobody, because the delegation was valid.","Only the other supplier, because the wholesaler handed over the duty.","The wholesaler, because delegating performance does not relieve the delegator of liability.","Nobody, because the shop never consented to the delegation."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-210, a duty to deliver ordinary goods can be delegated, but the delegation does not relieve the delegator of the duty to perform or of liability for breach. Delegate the chore, but you are liable as before.","id":"ucc-delegation-m1"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A shampoo maker appoints a firm as its exclusive distributor for a region, which carries a duty to use best efforts to sell the shampoo. The firm is then bought by a subsidiary of the maker's biggest competitor, which takes over the distributorship. The maker never consented. Under the majority view in Sally Beauty v. Nexxus, may the maker refuse the new distributor?","o":["Yes, because the maker has a substantial interest in not having a competitor perform the best-efforts duty.","No, because every duty under a sale of goods contract can be delegated.","No, because the maker can only sue the original firm for damages.","Yes, because distributorship contracts can never be transferred to anyone."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-210 bars delegation where the other party has a substantial interest in having the original promisor perform. In Sally Beauty, the court held that an exclusive distributor's best-efforts duty cannot be delegated to a competitor's subsidiary without consent.","id":"ucc-delegation-m2"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer of machine parts learns that its seller has delegated production to a firm the buyer has never heard of. The contract does not prohibit delegation. What may the buyer do under the UCC?","o":["Cancel at once, because any delegation is a breach.","Nothing at all until the parts arrive.","Refuse to pay the original seller and pay only the delegate.","Treat the delegation as reasonable grounds for insecurity and demand assurances from the delegate."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-210 lets the other party treat a delegation as creating reasonable grounds for insecurity. The buyer may then demand adequate assurances under section 2-609, without giving up its rights against the original seller. This is the tool Judge Posner pointed to.","id":"ucc-delegation-m3"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"both","q":"Which statement is true of delegation under both Restatement §318 and UCC §2-210?","o":["Delegation always requires the other party's written consent.","A duty may be delegated unless the other party has a substantial interest in the original person performing, and the delegator remains liable.","Once a duty is delegated, the delegator is automatically released.","Only duties to pay money can ever be delegated."],"a":1,"w":"The common law and the UCC are twins here. Under Restatement section 318 and UCC section 2-210, duties are generally delegable unless personal performance matters to the other side. Either way, the delegator stays on the hook unless there is a novation.","id":"ucc-delegation-m4"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A furniture maker delivers 30 tables to a hotel, which owes $9,000 in sixty days. The maker assigns its right to that payment to a bank. The hotel objects that it never agreed to deal with a bank. Is the assignment effective?","o":["No, because rights under a sale of goods contract are personal.","No, because the hotel did not consent.","Yes, because paying the bank instead of the maker does not materially change the hotel's duty, burden or risk.","Yes, but only if the bank also takes over the duty to deliver tables."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-210, rights can be assigned unless the assignment would materially change the other party's duty, or materially increase its burden or risk. Paying the same sum to a different payee changes nothing that matters. Remember D B R.","id":"ucc-delegation-m5"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A tyre wholesaler sends a retailer a signed letter: \"We offer 400 tyres at $60 each. This offer will be held open for 30 days.\" The retailer pays nothing for the promise. On day 10 the wholesaler tries to revoke. On day 12 the retailer accepts. Is there a contract?","o":["No, because the retailer gave no consideration for the 30-day promise.","Yes, because the letter was a firm offer that could not be revoked during the 30 days.","No, because an offer can always be revoked before acceptance.","Yes, but only if the retailer relied on the offer to its detriment."],"a":1,"w":"This is section 2-205. A merchant, a signed writing, and an assurance that the offer will be held open. SAM is all there, so the offer is irrevocable for the stated time without any consideration.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m1"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A merchant's signed written offer to sell goods states: \"This offer will remain open for eight months.\" No consideration is paid. For how long is the offer irrevocable under the UCC?","o":["Eight months, as stated.","A reasonable time, decided by the court.","Not at all, because the stated period is too long.","Three months."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-205 caps the period of irrevocability at three months. The firm part of the offer ends there. After that the offer may still be open, but the merchant is free to revoke it.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m2"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"both","q":"Dana, an accountant, writes and signs a note to her neighbour: \"I'll sell you my lawnmower for $150. I promise to keep this offer open until Sunday.\" On Friday, before the neighbour answers, Dana says she has changed her mind. Can she revoke?","o":["Yes, because she is not a merchant and received no consideration for her promise.","No, because the promise was in a signed writing.","No, because the UCC makes every written offer to sell goods irrevocable.","Yes, but only because a lawnmower costs less than $500."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-205 only covers offers made by merchants. Dana is not one, so the common-law rule applies: without consideration for an option, an offer can be revoked any time before acceptance, as in Dickinson and Dodds.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m3"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A paper supplier tells a print shop by phone, \"I'll sell you 100 reams at $4 each, and I'll keep that offer open all week.\" Nothing is put in writing. Is this a firm offer under the UCC?","o":["Yes, because the supplier is a merchant.","Yes, because the period is shorter than three months.","No, because a firm offer needs a signed writing.","No, because the price is under $500."],"a":2,"w":"A firm offer under section 2-205 must be in a signed writing that gives assurance it will be held open. An oral promise by a merchant is not enough, so the supplier can still revoke before acceptance.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m4"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer sends a seller the buyer's own pre-printed order form. Buried in the form is a clause: \"Seller agrees to hold this offer open for 45 days.\" The seller, a merchant, signs once at the bottom of the form. Is the hold-open clause binding as a firm offer?","o":["Yes, because one signature covers every clause on the form.","No, because the clause is on a form supplied by the offeree and was not separately signed by the seller.","Yes, because both parties are merchants.","No, because firm offers may never appear on a pre-printed form."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-205 says that an assurance on a form supplied by the offeree must be separately signed by the offeror. The rule protects merchants from being locked in by a clause they never noticed.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m5"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A shop orders 200 white mugs \"for prompt shipment.\" The seller, out of white, ships 200 green mugs with no explanation. What is the legal effect of the shipment?","o":["It is a counteroffer that the shop may accept or reject.","It has no effect, because the goods do not match the order.","It is both an acceptance of the order and a breach of the contract.","It is a rejection of the shop's offer."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-206, shipping non-conforming goods in response to an order for prompt shipment is an acceptance, and a breach at the same time. The seller could have avoided that by telling the shop the green mugs were only an accommodation.","id":"ucc-formation-m1"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"Two merchants sign a deal for 1,000 bolts and clearly intend to be bound, but say nothing about price. Under the UCC, what is the price?","o":["A reasonable price at the time for delivery.","Whatever price the seller names on the invoice.","The lowest price either party suggested in negotiations.","There is no price, so there is no contract."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-305 says that if the parties intend a contract and leave the price open, the price is a reasonable price at the time for delivery. Three-oh-five: the zero is a blank price tag, and the court fills it in.","id":"ucc-formation-m2"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"both","q":"Parties intend to be bound but leave the price \"to be agreed later,\" and then cannot agree. How do the traditional common-law approach and the UCC differ?","o":["Both treat the deal as unenforceable.","Both let the court supply a reasonable price in every case.","The common law supplies a price; the UCC refuses to.","The common law tends to call it an unenforceable agreement to agree; the UCC supplies a reasonable price."],"a":3,"w":"At common law, an agreement to agree on a key term like price is often too uncertain to enforce, as in the deli lease case. For goods, sections 2-204 and 2-305 keep the deal alive and fill the gap with a reasonable price, so long as the parties intended to be bound.","id":"ucc-formation-m3"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A cafe has a requirements contract for coffee beans and has always needed about 50 bags a month. When the market price doubles, it demands 900 bags so it can resell them at a profit. Must the seller deliver 900?","o":["Yes, because a requirements contract has no quantity limit.","No, because the demand is not in good faith and is unreasonably disproportionate to past requirements.","Yes, because the seller took the risk of price changes.","No, because requirements contracts are unenforceable for lack of a quantity."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-306 enforces requirements contracts, but the quantity means actual requirements in good faith, and nothing unreasonably disproportionate to an estimate or to normal past amounts. All I need, in good faith, without the greed.","id":"ucc-formation-m4"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A grower and a grocer never sign anything and cannot say exactly when they made their deal. For two years the grower has delivered apples weekly and the grocer has paid each invoice. Is there a contract for sale?","o":["No, because the moment of formation cannot be identified.","No, because a contract for goods always needs a signed offer and acceptance.","Yes, because conduct by both parties that recognises a contract is enough.","Yes, but only if a court can find a written purchase order."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-204 says a contract for goods may be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, including conduct, even if the moment of its making is undetermined. Agreement first, formalities second.","id":"ucc-formation-m5"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"both","q":"A buyer's purchase order for 500 valves is answered by a seller's acknowledgment that agrees on goods, price and quantity but adds a clause charging interest on late payments. Nothing is shipped yet. Is there a contract?","o":["Under both the common law and the UCC, yes.","Under both the common law and the UCC, no.","Under the common law mirror image rule, no; under UCC 2-207(1), yes.","Under the common law, yes; under the UCC, no."],"a":2,"w":"At common law the added clause makes the reply a rejection and counteroffer, because it does not mirror the offer. Under section 2-207, subsection one, a definite and timely expression of acceptance is an acceptance even though it states additional terms.","id":"ucc-forms-m1"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"Two merchants exchange forms and a contract is formed under 2-207(1). The seller's acceptance adds a new clause. The buyer's original purchase order stated: \"Acceptance is limited to the terms of this order.\" Does the seller's new clause become part of the contract?","o":["No, because the offer expressly limited acceptance to its own terms.","Yes, because both parties are merchants.","Yes, unless the clause materially alters the contract.","No, because additional terms never become part of a contract."],"a":0,"w":"Between merchants, additional terms come in unless L M O. Here it is the L: the offer expressly limited acceptance to its own terms, so under section 2-207, subsection two, the seller's proposal stays out.","id":"ucc-forms-m2"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A seller's acknowledgment states in bold: \"Our acceptance is expressly conditional on your assent to the terms on this form.\" The buyer never assents, but the seller ships and the buyer accepts and pays for the goods. What are the terms of the contract?","o":["The seller's terms, because its form was the last one sent.","The buyer's terms, because the buyer made the offer.","There is no contract, because the forms never matched.","The terms on which both forms agree, plus UCC gap-fillers."],"a":3,"w":"The expressly conditional language means the writings did not form a contract under subsection one. But conduct by both parties did. Under section 2-207, subsection three, the terms are those the writings agree on, plus the UCC's supplementary terms. No last shot.","id":"ucc-forms-m3"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A merchant buyer's form says the seller warrants the goods. The merchant seller's acceptance says all implied warranties are disclaimed. A contract is formed under 2-207(1). Under the majority \"knockout\" approach, what happens to the warranty terms?","o":["The seller's disclaimer controls as the later term.","Both conflicting terms drop out and the UCC's implied warranties fill the gap.","The buyer's term controls because the buyer was the offeror.","The whole contract fails for lack of agreement on warranties."],"a":1,"w":"Under the knockout rule, different terms that conflict cancel each other out, and the UCC's gap-fillers take their place. The gap-filler here is the implied warranty of merchantability. When terms collide, both step aside.","id":"ucc-forms-m4"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A consumer orders a sofa from a furniture store. The store's written acceptance agrees to the order and adds a clause requiring the consumer to pay a restocking fee on any return. The consumer says nothing about the clause. Is the restocking clause part of the contract?","o":["Yes, because the consumer did not object within a reasonable time.","Yes, because the clause does not materially alter the deal.","No, because with a non-merchant an additional term is only a proposal, which stays out unless agreed to.","No, because the store's reply was a counteroffer."],"a":2,"w":"The automatic rule for additional terms in section 2-207, subsection two, applies only between merchants. When one party is not a merchant, additional terms are just proposals, and they stay out of the contract unless that party actually agrees.","id":"ucc-forms-m5"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A seller and buyer orally agree on the sale of 300 lamps for $6,000. The only writing is a note signed by the buyer: \"Confirming our lamp deal — 120 lamps.\" The buyer later refuses to take any lamps. To what extent can the seller enforce the contract?","o":["For all 300 lamps, because the oral agreement controls.","Not at all, because the writing gets the quantity wrong.","Not at all, because the note does not state the price.","For 120 lamps, the quantity stated in the writing."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 2-201, subsection one, a writing is good enough even if it leaves out or misstates a term, but the contract is not enforceable beyond the quantity shown in the writing. The price can be missing. The quantity is the ceiling.","id":"ucc-frauds-m1"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"Two merchants agree by phone on the sale of $9,000 of steel. The next day the seller emails a signed confirmation stating the quantity. The buyer reads it and does nothing. Three weeks later the buyer denies the deal and raises the statute of frauds. Result?","o":["The defence fails, because the buyer did not object in writing within 10 days of receiving the confirmation.","The defence succeeds, because the buyer never signed anything.","The defence succeeds, because phone deals for goods are never enforceable.","The defence fails, because merchants are exempt from the statute of frauds."],"a":0,"w":"This is the merchant's confirmation rule in section 2-201, subsection two. Between merchants, a written confirmation that the recipient has reason to know about binds the recipient unless they object in writing within ten days. Ten days to say no way.","id":"ucc-frauds-m2"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A restaurant orally orders $3,000 of dinner plates stamped with its own crest. After the manufacturer has made half of them, the restaurant cancels and points out that nothing was signed. Can the manufacturer enforce the contract?","o":["No, because the price is over $500 and there is no signed writing.","Yes, because the goods are specially manufactured, unsuitable for sale to others, and substantially begun.","No, because only merchants can rely on oral contracts.","Yes, because restaurants are always bound by oral orders."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-201, subsection three, makes an exception for specially manufactured goods that cannot be sold to others in the ordinary course, once the seller has made a substantial beginning. That is the S in SAP.","id":"ucc-frauds-m3"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"both","q":"Which statement best compares the writing required by UCC 2-201 with the writing traditionally required by the common-law statute of frauds?","o":["They are identical: both need every term of the deal.","The UCC is stricter: the writing must state the price and the delivery date.","The UCC is more relaxed: the writing must show a contract, be signed, and state a quantity, even if other terms are missing.","The common law requires no signature, but the UCC does."],"a":2,"w":"The common-law statute of frauds generally looks for a signed memorandum stating the essential terms. Section 2-201 asks for much less: a signed writing showing that a contract for sale was made, with a quantity. Other terms may be missing or even wrong.","id":"ucc-frauds-m4"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer orally agrees to buy 10 bicycles for $2,000. The seller delivers 4 bicycles, which the buyer accepts and keeps. There is no writing. The buyer refuses the other 6. For how many bicycles is the contract enforceable?","o":["None, because there is no writing.","Only the 4 bicycles that were received and accepted.","All 10, because part performance proves the whole contract.","All 10, but only if the buyer is a merchant."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 2-201, subsection three, an oral contract is enforceable with respect to goods that have been received and accepted, or paid for with the payment accepted. The exception reaches only those goods, not the rest of the order.","id":"ucc-frauds-m5"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A seller contracts to sell 20 desks at $300 each ($6,000). The buyer repudiates before delivery. After notifying the buyer, the seller resells the desks in a commercially reasonable sale for $250 each ($5,000), and spends $200 storing them in the meantime. What may the seller recover?","o":["$1,000.","$1,200.","$6,000.","$800."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-706 gives the contract price minus the resale price, plus incidental damages. Six thousand minus five thousand is one thousand, and the two hundred dollars of storage is an incidental cost. Total: twelve hundred dollars.","id":"ucc-seller-m1"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A car dealer can get as many of a popular model as it has customers for. A buyer signs for one, then backs out. The next week the dealer sells that same car to a walk-in customer at the same price. The dealer's profit on each sale is $3,000. What is the dealer's best measure of damages?","o":["Nothing, because the resale price equalled the contract price.","The full contract price of the car.","Nothing, because the market price equalled the contract price.","Its $3,000 lost profit, plus incidental costs."],"a":3,"w":"This is a lost-volume seller, as in Neri and Retail Marine. The walk-in customer would have bought a car anyway, so the breach cost the dealer one sale. Section 2-708, subsection two, awards the lost profit when the market formula is inadequate.","id":"ucc-seller-m2"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A trophy maker engraves 200 trophies with a company's name and logo, exactly as ordered. The company then refuses to take them. Nobody else has any use for the trophies. What may the trophy maker recover?","o":["The full contract price, because the goods cannot reasonably be resold.","Only the contract price minus the scrap value of the metal, never the price.","Nothing, until it actually finds another buyer.","Only its lost profit, because the goods were never accepted."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-709 allows an action for the price in three cases: goods accepted, goods lost after the risk passed, and goods the seller cannot resell at a reasonable price after reasonable effort. Custom goods are the classic third case. Remember A L U.","id":"ucc-seller-m3"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"both","q":"A contract for $2,000 of garden furniture says that if the buyer cancels, the buyer must pay $50,000 as \"agreed damages.\" The buyer cancels, and the seller's real loss is about $300. Is the clause enforceable?","o":["Yes, because the buyer agreed to it.","Yes, because the UCC enforces every liquidated damages clause.","No, because it is unreasonably large, and so void as a penalty, under the UCC just as at common law.","No, because the UCC forbids all liquidated damages clauses."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-718 allows liquidated damages only at an amount that is reasonable in light of the anticipated or actual harm. A term fixing unreasonably large damages is void as a penalty. The common law draws the same line between a fair forecast and a punishment.","id":"ucc-seller-m4"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"Which seller's remedy is the mirror image of the buyer's right to \"cover\"?","o":["The action for the price under §2-709.","Resale under §2-706.","Liquidated damages under §2-718.","Stopping delivery under §2-703."],"a":1,"w":"A buyer covers by buying substitute goods and claiming the cover price minus the contract price. A seller does the reverse under section 2-706: resell the goods and claim the contract price minus the resale price. Seven-oh-six: resale is the fix.","id":"ucc-seller-m5"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A restaurant orders 50 white tablecloths, to be delivered by June 1. They arrive on May 20, and 10 of them are cream. The restaurant rejects the cream ones. The seller immediately says it will deliver 10 white ones by May 28. May the restaurant cancel the contract now?","o":["Yes, because the perfect tender rule lets a buyer cancel for any defect.","Yes, because the seller has had its one chance to perform.","No, because the time for performance has not expired and the seller has given notice that it will cure.","No, because a colour difference can never be a non-conformity."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-508 gives the seller a right to cure when the contract time has not yet run out, as long as it tells the buyer promptly. The rejection was fine under section 2-601, but the seller gets until June 1 to make a conforming delivery.","id":"ucc-tender-m1"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"both","q":"A builder finishes a house with one trivial deviation from the plans. Separately, a seller makes a single delivery of goods with one trivial deviation from the order. Which statement is correct?","o":["The owner must pay the builder, less damages, but the buyer of goods may reject the delivery.","Both the owner and the buyer may refuse to pay anything.","Both the owner and the buyer must accept and sue for damages.","The owner may refuse to pay, but the buyer of goods must accept."],"a":0,"w":"The common law uses substantial performance, so the builder gets paid minus damages. For goods, section 2-601 sets the perfect tender rule: a failure to conform in any respect lets the buyer reject, subject to the seller's right to cure.","id":"ucc-tender-m2"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A café has a contract for 12 monthly deliveries of coffee beans. The March delivery is two per cent short, and the supplier offers to send the missing beans the next day. May the café reject the March delivery?","o":["Yes, because any shortfall breaches the perfect tender rule.","Yes, but only if it also cancels the whole contract.","No, because a buyer may never reject part of an instalment contract.","No, because the shortfall does not substantially impair the instalment's value and it can be cured."],"a":3,"w":"This is an instalment contract, so section 2-612 replaces perfect tender. The buyer may reject an instalment only if the defect substantially impairs its value and cannot be cured. A small shortage that the seller will fix tomorrow does neither.","id":"ucc-tender-m3"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A print shop takes delivery of a new press, inspects it, and uses it every day for three months without complaint. It then notices a small scratch on a side panel that has been there since delivery, and tries to send the press back. Can it?","o":["Yes, because the scratch means the tender was never perfect.","No, because it accepted the press, and a minor defect does not substantially impair its value.","Yes, because a buyer can reject at any time before paying in full.","No, because machines are not goods under the UCC."],"a":1,"w":"By using the press after a chance to inspect, the shop accepted it under section 2-606, and acceptance ends the right to reject. Revocation under section 2-608 needs a defect that substantially impairs the value of the goods. A scratch does not.","id":"ucc-tender-m4"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"After a buyer has accepted goods, who bears the burden of proving that the goods did not conform to the contract?","o":["The seller, who must prove the goods were perfect.","Neither party; the court presumes a breach.","The buyer, who must establish the breach.","The carrier that delivered the goods."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-607, acceptance has consequences. The buyer must pay at the contract rate, must notify the seller of any breach within a reasonable time, and carries the burden of proving the defect. Inspect and object, or you accept.","id":"ucc-tender-m5"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"A court finds that a repossession clause in a contract for the sale of a tractor was unconscionable when the contract was made. Under UCC §2-302, which of these is NOT one of the court's options?","o":["Refuse to enforce the whole contract.","Enforce the rest of the contract without the clause.","Limit the clause so that it does not produce an unconscionable result.","Order the seller to pay punitive damages to the buyer."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-302 gives the court three choices: refuse the contract, strike the clause, or limit the clause. It is a shield, not a sword. Nothing in it awards punitive damages.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m1"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"Under UCC §2-302, who decides whether a clause is unconscionable, and as of what moment?","o":["The judge, looking at the time the contract was made.","The jury, looking at the time of trial.","The judge, looking at the time of the breach.","The jury, looking at the time the contract was made."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-302 makes unconscionability a matter of law for the court, tested at the time the contract was made. Judge, not jury. Signing day, not later fury. The parties do get to offer evidence of the commercial setting.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m2"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"A store sells a generator to a contractor for $4,000, a fair market price, after ordinary negotiation. Six months later a new model comes out and the market price of the old one falls to $1,500. The contractor, who still owes $3,000, says the price is unconscionable. Is it?","o":["Yes, because the price is now more than double the market value.","Yes, because the contractor has less bargaining power than the store.","No, because unconscionability is tested when the contract was made, and the deal was fair then.","No, because the UCC never lets a court review a price term."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-302 asks whether the term was unconscionable at the time it was made. A market that moves afterwards does not turn a fair deal into an unconscionable one. Note that courts can review price terms, as Frostifresh shows.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m3"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"How does UCC §2-302 relate to the common-law rule in Restatement §208?","o":["§2-302 applies to every contract, and §208 applies only to goods.","They give courts essentially the same power; §2-302 covers sales of goods and §208 covers other contracts.","§208 lets a court refuse enforcement, but §2-302 only allows damages.","They conflict, so a court must choose whichever favours the seller."],"a":1,"w":"The two rules are twins. Section 2-302 governs sales of goods, and Restatement section 208, which was modelled on it, does the same job for other contracts. Both let a court refuse or limit an unconscionable term.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m4"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"A salesman rushes a buyer through a sofa contract, covering the page with his hand and saying, \"That's just the usual stuff.\" The hidden text is in tiny print. Which kind of unconscionability do these facts mainly show?","o":["Procedural, because the problem lies in how the bargain was made.","Substantive, because the terms are hidden.","Neither, because the buyer signed the contract.","Substantive, because sofas are consumer goods."],"a":0,"w":"Procedural unconscionability is about an unfair process: hidden terms, pressure, and no real choice. Substantive unconscionability is about the terms themselves being harsh. Remember P S, and that most courts look for some of each under section 2-302.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m5"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A camping store's label on a tent reads \"Waterproof in heavy rain.\" The salesperson adds, \"This is the finest tent money can buy.\" The tent leaks in the first storm. Which statement created an express warranty?","o":["Both statements.","Only \"Waterproof in heavy rain.\"","Only \"the finest tent money can buy.\"","Neither, because the word \"warranty\" was never used."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 2-313, an affirmation of fact or a description that is part of the basis of the bargain is an express warranty, and no magic words are needed. Calling it the finest tent money can buy is opinion, or puffery, which is not a warranty.","id":"ucc-warranty-m1"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A painter tells a hardware store clerk, \"I need paint for a metal boat hull that sits in salt water. What should I use?\" The clerk selects an ordinary house paint, which is perfectly good on walls but peels off the hull in a week. The painter's best claim is breach of:","o":["The warranty of title.","The implied warranty of merchantability only.","An express warranty created by puffery.","The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-315 applies when the seller has reason to know the buyer's particular purpose and that the buyer is relying on the seller's skill to select the goods. The paint may be merchantable as house paint, yet still unfit for the hull. Three-fifteen: tell the seller what you mean.","id":"ucc-warranty-m2"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A dentist sells her used family blender to a neighbour and says nothing about its condition. It stops working after one day. Is there a breach of the implied warranty of merchantability?","o":["No, because the warranty arises only when the seller is a merchant in goods of that kind.","Yes, because all goods must be fit for their ordinary purpose.","Yes, because the dentist is a professional.","No, because blenders are not goods."],"a":0,"w":"The implied warranty of merchantability in section 2-314 arises only if the seller is a merchant with respect to goods of that kind. A dentist making a one-off sale of a blender is not a merchant in blenders.","id":"ucc-warranty-m3"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A dealer's written sales contract contains, in the same small print as everything else on the back page, the sentence: \"Seller disclaims all implied warranties.\" Is the implied warranty of merchantability effectively disclaimed?","o":["Yes, because any written disclaimer works.","Yes, because the buyer signed the contract.","No, because a written disclaimer of merchantability must mention merchantability and be conspicuous.","No, because implied warranties can never be disclaimed."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-316 says a disclaimer of merchantability must mention the word merchantability, and if it is in writing it must be conspicuous. This one does neither, and it is not an as is clause. Three-sixteen: disclaimers must be seen.","id":"ucc-warranty-m4"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"both","q":"Which statement best contrasts the old common-law attitude to the quality of goods with the UCC's approach?","o":["The common law implied broad warranties; the UCC abolished them.","The common law followed caveat emptor; the UCC adds implied warranties to sales unless they are properly disclaimed.","Both leave quality entirely to what the seller chooses to promise.","The UCC applies caveat emptor to merchants and implied warranties to everyone else."],"a":1,"w":"Caveat emptor means let the buyer beware: no promise about quality unless the seller made one. The UCC reverses the starting point. Sections 2-314 and 2-315 add implied warranties by law, and section 2-316 sets the rules a seller must follow to disclaim them.","id":"ucc-warranty-m5"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"Which of these is the best example of PROCEDURAL unconscionability?","o":["A price three times the market value.","A harsh clause in tiny print, never explained to a signer who could not read the language of the contract.","A clause that lets only one side cancel.","A late fee ten times larger than any real loss."],"a":1,"w":"Procedural unconscionability is about how the deal was made: hidden terms, no real choice, no real understanding. The other three options are about what the terms say, which is substantive. Sneaky how, nasty what.","id":"unconscionability-m1"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"A door-to-door seller sells a sound system that cost him $300 to a couple for $1,500 on credit. The sales talk was in Portuguese, the only language the couple reads. The contract was in English and was never translated. The seller sues for the full price. What is the couple's best argument?","o":["The contract is void because consumer sales must be in the buyer's language.","The seller made a mutual mistake about the price.","The price term is unconscionable, so under UCC §2-302 the court may refuse to enforce it.","The seller gave no consideration."],"a":2,"w":"This is the freezer case, Frostifresh and Reynoso, in new clothes. A sound system is goods, so the rule is UCC section 2-302, the twin of Restatement section 208. An excessive price plus a language barrier gives both substantive and procedural unfairness.","id":"unconscionability-m2"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"Two large companies, each advised by lawyers, negotiate a service contract for months. A year later one of them realises it agreed to pay about 15% more than the going rate. Is the contract unconscionable?","o":["No. A hard bargain between sophisticated parties with real choice is still enforceable.","Yes, because any price above the market rate is substantively unfair.","Yes, because the company would not have signed if it had known.","No, because unconscionability applies only to sales of goods."],"a":0,"w":"A bad deal is not an unconscionable deal. There was no unfair process here, and fifteen percent over market does not shock the conscience. Section 208 is a safety valve, not a refund counter.","id":"unconscionability-m3"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement §208, as of what moment does a court ask whether a contract or term is unconscionable?","o":["When the lawsuit is filed.","When the breach happens.","When the judge hears the case.","When the contract was made."],"a":3,"w":"Section 208 asks whether the term was unconscionable at the time the contract was made. The court does not use hindsight. A fair deal that later turns out badly is just a deal that turned out badly.","id":"unconscionability-m4"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"A court finds that one clause in an otherwise fair contract is unconscionable. Under Restatement §208, which of these may the court do?","o":["Nothing. A signed contract must be enforced as written.","Enforce the rest of the contract without the unconscionable clause.","Award punitive damages against the party who drafted the clause.","Rewrite the entire contract on terms the judge thinks are fair."],"a":1,"w":"Section 208 gives the court three tools. It may refuse to enforce the whole contract, enforce the rest without the bad term, or limit the term so the result is not unconscionable. Refuse, strike, or limit.","id":"unconscionability-m5"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 177, undue influence is best described as:","o":["An improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative.","Unfair persuasion of a person who is dominated by the persuader or justified in trusting them.","A false statement of fact that the victim relies on.","A contract term so one-sided that it shocks the conscience."],"a":1,"w":"Section 177 has two ingredients: unfair persuasion, and a weak spot of domination or trust. Picture one seven seven: one tired person with two sevens leaning over him. The first option is duress, which is a different defence.","id":"undue-m1"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"An elderly, fully competent widow relies on her financial adviser for every money decision. The adviser persuades her to sell him her lake cabin for a third of its value. She took no outside advice. Her family sues to undo the sale. What is the likely analysis?","o":["The sale stands, because she was mentally competent.","The sale stands, because the adviser made no threat.","The sale is void, because advisers may never buy from clients.","A presumption of undue influence arises, and the adviser must show the deal was fair."],"a":3,"w":"This is In re Estate of Burren. When a fiduciary benefits from a deal with the person who trusts him, undue influence is presumed and the fiduciary must rebut it. Competence does not save the deal, and no threat is needed.","id":"undue-m2"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the warning signs of over-persuasion listed in Odorizzi v. Bloomfield School District?","o":["The agreement was put in writing.","Several persuaders worked on one person.","The deal was discussed at an unusual time.","The person was told there was no time to consult a lawyer."],"a":0,"w":"Run through mean tip. Multiple persuaders, emphasis on delay, advisers absent, no time for a lawyer, unusual time, insistence on finishing now, unusual place. Putting a deal in writing is not on the list.","id":"undue-m3"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"At 11 p.m. on the night of an accident, two insurance adjusters visit the injured driver in his hospital room. They say the offer expires tonight and there is no point calling a lawyer. Groggy, he signs a release for a small sum. No threat is made. What is his best defence?","o":["Duress, because he was in hospital.","Lack of capacity, because all patients lack capacity.","Undue influence, because of the over-persuasion of a weakened person.","None, because he signed voluntarily."],"a":2,"w":"Count the Odorizzi signs: unusual time, unusual place, insistence on finishing at once, multiple persuaders, no advisers, no time for a lawyer. There is no threat, so duress is a poor fit. Undue influence is the natural claim.","id":"undue-m4"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"A landlord tells a tenant, \"Sign this new lease at double the rent or I will smash your shop windows tonight.\" The tenant signs. Which defence fits best, and why?","o":["Undue influence, because the landlord used unfair persuasion.","Duress, because the landlord made an improper threat.","Undue influence, because landlords are fiduciaries.","Neither, because the tenant signed the lease."],"a":1,"w":"Duress twists your arm. Undue influence bends your ear. A threat to commit a crime or tort is the classic improper threat for duress. Undue influence is about over-persuasion within domination or trust, with no threat required.","id":"undue-m5"}];
